\id REV - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Revelation
\toc1 The Unveiling of Jesus the Christ
\toc2 Revelation
\toc3 Rev
\mt1 Revelation

\imt Introduction
\ip A man named John, held on the small Aegean island of Patmos for what he had said about God and about Jesus, writes to seven assemblies in the Roman province of Asia — the western edge of what is now Turkey. He names them: Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamum, Thyatira, Sardis, Philadelphia, Laodicea. They lie on a road that loops north from Ephesus and back round, in the order a courier would walk them, and every assembly hears all seven letters. This is not a private journal of visions. It is a circular letter, meant to be read aloud in the meeting, and it says so in its third sentence. There were more than seven assemblies in Asia; seven is the number of a complete set, so these seven stand for the whole.
\ip The pressure on them came from several directions at once. Rome had made the worship of the emperor the civic glue of the province — temples, festivals, oaths, and the trade guilds whose dinners were held in temple precincts and opened with an offering. A man who would not take part could lose his guild, and with it his living. Local synagogues, which had a legal exemption from that worship, had begun denouncing the Jesus-followers as no part of them, which left them exposed. At least one man, Antipas of Pergamum, had already been killed. And inside the assemblies were teachers arguing that the meat and the dinners were nothing, that a believer could go along and stay clean. Two assemblies were rich and comfortable; one was already dead and did not know it.
\ip What John sends them is written almost entirely out of Israel's scriptures. There is scarcely an image in the book that is not lifted from Daniel, Ezekiel, Isaiah, Zechariah, Joel, the Exodus, or the Psalms, and its first hearers decoded it by recognizing the source. When they heard "one like a son of man" they heard Daniel 7; when they heard hail and blood and darkness they heard the plagues on Egypt; when they heard "Babylon" they heard the empire that burned the temple. Most of the notes below are that lost common knowledge put back. John also warns, in his opening line, that he has put the message into *signs* — these are pictures pointing at realities, not descriptions of them.
\ip The claim the book makes is a political one. Rome looks permanent and looks divine; John shows it as a beast riding out of the sea on borrowed power, carrying a drunk and doomed woman, its number falling three times short of seven. The throne that actually governs is not in Rome, and the one who holds it conquered by being slaughtered. The date is disputed — most place it in the nineties, under Domitian, some in the late sixties — but the situation is the same either way. To assemblies who are being squeezed and will be squeezed harder, the book says: this is what is really happening, this is how it ends, and your part is to hold out.

\c 1
\s1 What This Book Is and Where It Came From
\p
\v 1 The unveiling\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl apokalypsis\tl*, the pulling back of a cover from something that was there all along. Not a code to be cracked but a curtain drawn aside.\f* of Jesus the Christ, which God gave him to show his slaves\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl douloi\tl*. In Israel's scriptures this is a title of honor, not shame — Moses, David, and the prophets are each called the slave of the LORD (Deuteronomy 34:5; 2 Samuel 7:5; Amos 3:7). It is this book's ordinary word for God's people.\f* what must happen quickly\f + \fr 1:1 \ft "What must happen" is Daniel's phrase (Daniel 2:28-29), where it was assigned to "the latter days." John adds "quickly." What Daniel was shown at a distance, John says is pressing on his own readers.\f*; and he made it known \add [i.e. put it into signs]\add*\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl esēmanen\tl*, from \tl sēmeion\tl*, a sign. The word is an instruction about how to read everything that follows: these are signs pointing at realities, not photographs of them.\f* by sending it through his angel to his slave John,
\v 2 who testified to the word of God and to the testimony of Jesus the Christ — to everything he saw.\f + \fr 1:2 \ft "The word of God and the testimony of Jesus" is one of this book's fixed pairs. It names what John is on Patmos for (1:9), what the slaughtered under the altar died for (6:9), what the dragon hunts men down over (12:17), and what the beheaded held to (20:4). It is, in short, what gets a person killed.\f*
\v 3 Blessed is the one who reads it aloud \add [i.e. one man reading to the gathered assembly]\add*, and blessed are those who hear the words of the prophecy and keep\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Greek \tl tēreō\tl*, to guard and hold — the covenant word of Deuteronomy 4:2, "you shall keep the commandments." The book is not offered as information to be pondered but as instruction to be obeyed.\f* the things written in it, for the time is near.\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Greek \tl kairos\tl*, not the passing of time but the arrival of the decisive moment.\f*
\s1 Greeting to the Seven Assemblies
\p
\v 4 John, to the seven assemblies in Asia\f + \fr 1:4 \ft Named in verse 11. There were more than seven assemblies in the province — Colossae, Hierapolis, and Troas are all attested — so the seven are a chosen set, and seven in this book is the number of a complete whole. These seven stand for all.\f*: favor to you and peace from the One who is and who was and who is coming\f + \fr 1:4 \ft An expansion of the name God gave Moses at the bush, "I am the one who is" (Exodus 3:14). The Aramaic paraphrases stretched it across time as "who is, who was, and who will be"; John changes the last term. God is not merely going to go on existing — he is on his way.\f*, and from the seven spirits before his throne,\f + \fr 1:4 \ft Zechariah saw a golden lampstand with seven lamps and was told, "These seven are the eyes of the LORD, ranging through the whole earth" (Zechariah 4:10); Isaiah described the Spirit resting on the coming king in a sevenfold way (Isaiah 11:2). The seven spirits are God's own Spirit in its fullness, and in 5:6 they turn out to be the Lamb's eyes.\f*
\v 5 and from Jesus the Christ, the faithful witness\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Greek \tl martys\tl*, a witness — the word from which "martyr" comes, and this book is where the shift begins. Antipas gets the same title in 2:13, and he is dead.\f*, the firstborn of the dead, and the ruler of the kings of the earth\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Psalm 89:27, of the Davidic king: "I will make him the firstborn, the highest of the kings of the earth." Applied to Jesus it is not a compliment but a counter-claim, addressed to assemblies living under a man who called himself lord of the world.\f*. To the one who loves us and loosed us from our sins by his blood —
\v 6 and he made us a kingdom, priests to his God and Father\f + \fr 1:6 \ft Exodus 19:6, God's word to Israel at Sinai after he had brought them out through the blood of the Passover lamb: "you shall be to me a kingdom of priests." The sequence is the same both times — loved, freed by blood, then made a kingdom of priests. What God did at the exodus he has done again.\f* — to him be the glory and the might into the ages of the ages. Amen.
\v 7 Look, he is coming with the clouds\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Two prophets welded together. The coming with clouds is Daniel 7:13, the human figure who is brought before the Ancient of Days and given everlasting rule. The piercing and the mourning are Zechariah 12:10, where Jerusalem looks on the one they pierced and grieves as for an only son. The one who comes is identifiable by what was done to him.\f*, and every eye will see him, even those who pierced him; and all the tribes of the earth will beat their breasts over him. Yes, amen.\add [i.e. yes in Greek and amen in Hebrew — the assembly answering in both its languages]\add*
\v 8 "I am the Alpha and the Omega\f + \fr 1:8 \ft The first and last letters of the Greek alphabet — Isaiah's "I am the first and I am the last; besides me there is no God" (Isaiah 44:6) put into the alphabet of the readers. In 22:13 Jesus will claim the same title.\f*," says the Lord God, "the One who is and who was and who is coming, the Lord of hosts.\f + \fr 1:8 \ft Greek \tl pantokratōr\tl*, the Greek scriptures' rendering of "LORD of hosts" and of Shaddai — the one who holds all power. It occurs nine times in this book and nowhere else so densely. Under an emperor addressed as lord and god, naming Israel's God the Almighty is not a description but a denial.\f*"
\s1 John Sees the Risen Christ Among the Lampstands
\p
\v 9 I, John, your brother and partner in the pressure\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Greek \tl thlipsis\tl*, the squeeze of hostile circumstance. One article in the Greek governs all three nouns — pressure, kingdom, and endurance are not three stages but one condition. To be in the kingdom now is to be under pressure, and that requires holding out.\f* and the kingdom and the endurance in Jesus, came to be on the island called Patmos\f + \fr 1:9 \ft A small rocky island off the coast of Asia. Rome used islands for banishment — a sentence short of execution, used on men judged dangerous but not worth killing. John is there because of what he said, not to get away and pray.\f* because of the word of God and the testimony of Jesus.
\v 10 I came to be in spirit\f + \fr 1:10 \ft The prophet's condition when the visions come. Ezekiel says the same of himself: "the Spirit lifted me up," "he brought me out in the Spirit of the LORD" (Ezekiel 3:12, 14; 37:1). The phrase marks four turning points in this book — here, 4:2, 17:3, and 21:10.\f* on the Lord's day, and I heard behind me a great voice like a trumpet\f + \fr 1:10 \ft The trumpet is the sound of God coming down. At Sinai "the sound of the trumpet grew louder and louder" as he descended (Exodus 19:16, 19); in Joel 2:1 it announces the day of the LORD.\f*
\v 11 saying, "What you see, write in a scroll and send it to the seven assemblies: to Ephesus and to Smyrna and to Pergamum and to Thyatira and to Sardis and to Philadelphia and to Laodicea."\f + \fr 1:11 \ft The order is a road. Beginning at the port of Ephesus it runs north up the coast to Smyrna and Pergamum, then inland and south-east through Thyatira, Sardis, Philadelphia, and Laodicea, and back toward Ephesus — the circuit a letter-carrier would walk.\f*
\v 12 And I turned to see the voice that was speaking with me; and having turned I saw seven golden lampstands,\f + \fr 1:12 \ft The lampstand is the menorah of the tabernacle (Exodus 25:31-40), beaten from one piece of gold, standing in the holy place. Verse 20 will say the seven lampstands are the seven assemblies. They are not visitors to the sanctuary; they are its furniture.\f*
\v 13 and in the middle of the lampstands one like a son of man\f + \fr 1:13 \ft Daniel 7:13, word for word: the human-looking figure who comes with the clouds and receives a dominion that will not pass away.\f*, clothed down to the feet and belted around the chest with a golden belt.\f + \fr 1:13 \ft A robe to the feet is the high priest's garment (Exodus 28:4), and a belt worn high across the chest rather than at the waist is the mark of dignity rather than of work. He is dressed as priest and as king at once.\f*
\v 14 His head and his hair were white like white wool, like snow\f + \fr 1:14 \ft This is not the son of man in Daniel's vision — it is the Ancient of Days, God himself, whose "clothing was white as snow and the hair of his head like pure wool" (Daniel 7:9). John gives the son of man the hair of God. The merger says in a picture what a sentence would take a page to say.\f*, and his eyes like a flame of fire,
\v 15 and his feet like burnished bronze fired in a furnace, and his voice like the sound of many waters,\f + \fr 1:15 \ft Fiery eyes and glowing bronze limbs come from Daniel 10:6. The voice like many waters is Ezekiel 43:2, where it belongs to the glory of the God of Israel returning to his temple.\f*
\v 16 and holding in his right hand seven stars, and out of his mouth a sharp two-edged sword going out\f + \fr 1:16 \ft His weapon is his speech. "He made my mouth like a sharp sword" (Isaiah 49:2); "he will strike the earth with the rod of his mouth" (Isaiah 11:4). This is the sword he uses on Pergamum (2:16) and on the nations at the end (19:15, 21) — he does not swing it, he speaks.\f*, and his face like the sun shining in its power.
\v 17 And when I saw him I fell at his feet like a dead man.\f + \fr 1:17 \ft The standard collapse of the prophet: Ezekiel "fell on my face" (Ezekiel 1:28), Daniel "fell prostrate" and lay as in a deep sleep (Daniel 8:17-18; 10:9), and in both a hand raises them.\f* And he laid his right hand on me, saying, "Do not be afraid. I am the first and the last,
\v 18 and the living one. I became dead, and look — I am living into the ages of the ages\add [i.e. I went into death as a man goes into a country, and came out]\add*, and I hold the keys of Death and of Hades.\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Whoever holds the keys controls who goes in and who comes out (Isaiah 22:22). Hades is the realm of the dead, the Greek scriptures' word for Sheol. Rome's whole authority rested on its power to kill; the one speaking has been inside that door and come back with the key in his hand.\f*
\v 19 Write, then, what you saw, and what is, and what is about to happen after these things.
\v 20 The secret\f + \fr 1:20 \ft Greek \tl mystērion\tl*, not something kept hidden but something whose meaning has to be disclosed before it can be read. Here the disclosure is given at once.\f* of the seven stars that you saw in my right hand, and the seven golden lampstands: the seven stars are the angels of the seven assemblies\f + \fr 1:20 \ft Greek \tl angeloi\tl*, messengers. The word covers heavenly beings and human envoys alike, and the book does not say which is meant. What it does say is where they are: in his hand.\f*, and the seven lampstands are the seven assemblies.

\c 2
\s1 To Ephesus: Right in Everything but Love
\p
\v 1 "To the angel of the assembly in Ephesus\f + \fr 2:1 \ft The largest city of the province, a great port, home of the temple of Artemis. Paul had worked there some three years and warned its elders at parting that "fierce wolves will come in among you" and that men from among themselves would rise up speaking twisted things (Acts 20:29-31). This assembly took him at his word.\f* write: These things says the one who holds the seven stars in his right hand, the one who walks in the middle of the seven golden lampstands:
\v 2 I know your works, and your labor and your endurance, and that you cannot bear evil men; and you tested those who call themselves sent ones and are not, and you found them liars.\f + \fr 2:2 \ft Travelling teachers claiming apostolic authority were a live problem, and testing them was a duty. A near-contemporary church manual sets a rough rule: an apostle who stays more than two days is a false prophet (Didache 11:4-5).\f*
\v 3 And you have endurance, and you bore up because of my name, and you have not grown weary.
\v 4 But I have this against you: that you have left your first love\add [i.e. you set it down and walked away from it]\add*.\f + \fr 2:4 \ft The complaint is Jeremiah's against Israel: "I remember the devotion of your youth, your love as a bride, how you followed me in the wilderness" (Jeremiah 2:2) — and then, "what wrong did your fathers find in me, that they went far from me?" The remedy in the next verse ties the love to works: whatever it was, it used to show.\f*
\v 5 Remember, then, from where you have fallen, and turn back and do the first works. If not, I am coming to you and I will move your lampstand from its place\add [i.e. this assembly will stop being a light-bearer where it stands]\add* — unless you turn back.
\v 6 But you have this: that you hate the works of the Nicolaitans, which I also hate.\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Named again at Pergamum (2:15), where their teaching is set beside Balaam's — eating what was offered to idols, and whoring. They are the party of accommodation: go to the guild dinner, the god is nothing anyway.\f*
\v 7 Let the one who has an ear hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies\add [i.e. to all seven, not this one only]\add*. To the one who conquers\f + \fr 2:7 \ft Greek \tl ho nikōn\tl*, a present participle: the one who is conquering, keeping at it. Every one of the seven letters ends with this promise, and 12:11 says how it is done — "by the blood of the Lamb and by the word of their testimony, and they did not love their life all the way to death." Conquest in this book means refusing to give in, not winning a fight.\f* I will give to eat from the tree of life, which is in the paradise of God.\f + \fr 2:7 \ft The tree in the middle of the garden, barred to man after the first disobedience and guarded by cherubim and a turning sword (Genesis 2:9; 3:22-24). What was shut at the beginning of the story is opened at its end (22:2).\f*
\s1 To Smyrna: Poor, Slandered, and About to Suffer
\p
\v 8 "And to the angel of the assembly in Smyrna\f + \fr 2:8 \ft A prosperous coastal city thirty-five miles north of Ephesus, fiercely loyal to Rome — it had won the competition to build the province's temple to Tiberius. It had also been destroyed centuries earlier and rebuilt: a city that died and came to life, addressed by one who did the same.\f* write: These things says the first and the last, who became dead and came to life:
\v 9 I know your pressure and your poverty — but you are rich — and the blasphemy from those who say they are Jews and are not, but are a synagogue of Satan.\f + \fr 2:9 \ft Hebrew \tl satan\tl* means the accuser, the adversary — the figure who brings charges against God's servants in Job 1-2 and Zechariah 3. The charge is not against Jews as such but against a particular group here whose denunciations were feeding the assembly to the authorities. Their claim to the name is denied on the ground of what they are doing with it. Two generations later the Jewish community of Smyrna would help gather wood for Polycarp's pyre.\f*
\v 10 Do not fear what you are about to suffer. Look, the devil is about to throw some of you into prison so that you may be tested, and you will have pressure ten days\f + \fr 2:10 \ft Daniel and his companions asked to be tested ten days on vegetables and water rather than eat the king's food (Daniel 1:12-14). The number marks a trial that is real and that has an end.\f*. Become faithful all the way to death, and I will give you the wreath of life.\f + \fr 2:10 \ft Greek \tl stephanos\tl*, the victor's garland, not a royal crown. Smyrna had games, and the ring of public buildings along the crest of its acropolis was known as the crown of Smyrna. They are promised a crown their city cannot award.\f*
\v 11 Let the one who has an ear hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies. The one who conquers will never be harmed by the second death.\f + \fr 2:11 \ft Defined later in the book as the lake of fire (20:14; 21:8). To an assembly facing execution the arithmetic is laid out plainly: the first death is what your persecutors can do; the second is what they cannot touch you with.\f*
\s1 To Pergamum: Faithful Where Satan's Throne Is, and Tolerating Balaam
\p
\v 12 "And to the angel of the assembly in Pergamum write: These things says the one who has the sharp two-edged sword:\f + \fr 2:12 \ft Pergamum was the seat of Roman government for the province, and the governor there held the right of the sword — the power to execute. The one dictating this letter names himself by a sword of his own.\f*
\v 13 I know where you dwell — where the throne of Satan is\f + \fr 2:13 \ft Pergamum's acropolis carried the huge altar of Zeus, visible for miles; the city had built the province's first temple to a living emperor, in 29 BC; and the shrine of Asclepius, whose emblem was a serpent, drew pilgrims from across the Mediterranean. The phrase need not pick one of these. The whole place was a throne room for what opposes God.\f* — and you hold fast my name, and you did not deny your trust in me even in the days of Antipas my faithful witness, who was killed among you, where Satan dwells.\f + \fr 2:13 \ft Antipas is given the same title as Jesus in 1:5, "faithful witness," and the title has already cost him his life.\f*
\v 14 But I have a few things against you: that you have there some holding the teaching of Balaam, who taught Balak to throw a stumbling block before the sons of Israel — to eat things sacrificed to idols and to commit whoring.\f + \fr 2:14 \ft Balaam could not curse Israel, so for a fee he showed Balak how to bring them down from inside: Moabite women, their sacrificial meals, and Baal of Peor (Numbers 25:1-3; 31:16). The two acts named here are exactly the two the Jerusalem council had ruled out for gentile believers (Acts 15:29). In a guild city they were not abstractions — the dinners were held in temples and the food had been offered.\f*
\v 15 So you also have some holding the teaching of the Nicolaitans in the same way.
\v 16 Turn back, then. If not, I am coming to you quickly, and I will make war against them with the sword of my mouth.\add [i.e. against them, not against you — but if you will not deal with them, I will, and no one there will enjoy it]\add*
\v 17 Let the one who has an ear hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies. To the one who conquers I will give some of the hidden manna\f + \fr 2:17 \ft The bread God gave in the wilderness, a jar of which was laid up before the ark (Exodus 16:32-34). A tradition held that Jeremiah hid the ark and its contents before Babylon came, "until God gathers his people together again" (2 Maccabees 2:4-8). To men under pressure to eat at pagan tables, the answer is: there is food kept back for you.\f*, and I will give him a white stone, and on the stone a new name written that no one knows except the one receiving it.\f + \fr 2:17 \ft A white pebble acquitted a man in court; a token like it admitted a guest to a feast or a member to an association. Refuse the guild's dinner and you are handed a different token, to a different table, by a different judge.\f*
\s1 To Thyatira: Growing, and Letting Jezebel Teach
\p
\v 18 "And to the angel of the assembly in Thyatira\f + \fr 2:18 \ft A smaller manufacturing town, thick with trade guilds — wool, linen, leather, bronze, pottery, baking, dyeing. Lydia the purple-seller of Acts 16:14 came from here. Guild membership was how a man ate, and guild membership meant the guild's dinners and the guild's god.\f* write: These things says the Son of God, the one whose eyes are like a flame of fire and whose feet are like burnished bronze:
\v 19 I know your works and the love and the trust and the service and your endurance, and that your last works are more than the first.\add [i.e. this assembly is not declining; it is growing]\add*
\v 20 But I have this against you: that you tolerate the woman Jezebel\f + \fr 2:20 \ft Almost certainly not her name but her likeness. Jezebel of Sidon, married to Ahab, did not force Baal on Israel at swordpoint; she brought him in through the court, fed his prophets at her table, and made him respectable (1 Kings 16:31-33; 18:4, 19). That is the charge: not assault from outside but normalization from within.\f*, who calls herself a prophetess, and she teaches and leads my slaves astray to commit whoring and to eat things sacrificed to idols.
\v 21 And I gave her time to turn back, and she does not want to turn back from her whoring.\add [i.e. the door was held open and she would not walk through it]\add*
\v 22 Look, I am throwing her onto a bed\add [i.e. she reclined at their feasts; she will lie down sick]\add*, and those committing adultery with her into great pressure, unless they turn back from her works.
\v 23 And I will kill her children with death; and all the assemblies will know that I am the one who searches kidneys and hearts\f + \fr 2:23 \ft Jeremiah 17:10, God speaking of himself: "I the LORD search the heart and test the kidneys, to give to every man according to his ways." In Hebrew the kidneys are the seat of the innermost self. The risen Christ takes the sentence over unaltered, including its ending.\f*, and I will give to each of you according to your works.
\v 24 But to you I say — to the rest in Thyatira, as many as do not hold this teaching, who have not known the deep things of Satan, as they call them\add [i.e. the deep things, they say; whose deep things, I say]\add* — I am not throwing any other burden on you;\f + \fr 2:24 \ft The wording echoes the Jerusalem council's ruling, "to lay on you no greater burden than these requirements" (Acts 15:28). They have already kept it. Nothing further is asked of them.\f*
\v 25 only hold fast what you have until I come.
\v 26 And the one who conquers and keeps my works to the end — to him I will give authority over the nations,
\v 27 and he will shepherd them with an iron staff, as the clay vessels are shattered,\f + \fr 2:27 \ft Psalm 2:8-9, spoken to God's anointed king. The Greek scriptures read "shepherd" where the Hebrew reads "break," and the iron staff and the smashed pottery make plain which kind of shepherding is meant. What was promised to the Messiah is here handed on to whoever holds out.\f*
\v 28 as I also have received from my Father; and I will give him the morning star.\f + \fr 2:28 \ft In 22:16 Jesus says, "I am the bright morning star." What is promised here is himself. Behind it stands Balaam's own oracle — the one prophecy of his that stood: "a star shall come out of Jacob" (Numbers 24:17). The teaching of Balaam is answered with the star of Balaam.\f*
\v 29 Let the one who has an ear hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies.

\c 3
\s1 To Sardis: A Name for Being Alive
\p
\v 1 "And to the angel of the assembly in Sardis write: These things says the one who has the seven spirits of God and the seven stars: I know your works — that you have a name that you are living, and you are dead.\add [i.e. a reputation, and nothing behind it]\add*
\v 2 Become watchful, and strengthen the things that remain, which were about to die; for I have not found your works fulfilled before my God.\add [i.e. begun and never carried through]\add*
\v 3 Remember, then, how you have received and heard, and keep it, and turn back. If, then, you do not stay awake, I will come like a thief, and you will never know what hour I will come upon you.\f + \fr 3:3 \ft Sardis sat on a crag its people thought unclimbable, and had been taken by surprise twice for exactly that reason — by Cyrus in 546 BC and by Antiochus in 214 BC, both times by night, both times by men who scaled the rock while the walls went unwatched. The city knew what this threat meant.\f*
\v 4 But you have a few names in Sardis that did not defile their garments\add [i.e. a few persons — soiled clothes stand for a life that has been compromised]\add*, and they will walk with me in white, because they are worthy.
\v 5 The one who conquers will in this way be clothed in white garments, and I will never wipe his name out of the scroll of life\f + \fr 3:5 \ft The book in which God keeps the names of his own. Moses says, "blot me out of your book," and God answers, "whoever has sinned against me, I will blot him out of my book" (Exodus 32:32-33); the psalmist prays that his enemies "be blotted out of the book of the living" (Psalm 69:28). The promise here is worth something only because the thing it denies is a real possibility.\f*, and I will confess his name before my Father and before his angels.\f + \fr 3:5 \ft The other half of a saying the assemblies knew: "Everyone who confesses me before men, I will also confess before my Father; but whoever denies me before men, I will also deny" (Matthew 10:32-33).\f*
\v 6 Let the one who has an ear hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies.
\s1 To Philadelphia: Little Power, an Open Door
\p
\v 7 "And to the angel of the assembly in Philadelphia write: These things says the holy one, the true one, the one who has the key of David, who opens and no one will shut, and shuts and no one opens:\f + \fr 3:7 \ft Isaiah 22:22, of Eliakim, who replaced the faithless steward Shebna: "I will place on his shoulder the key of the house of David; he shall open and none shall shut." The steward with the key decides who is admitted to the king. To an assembly being shut out of the synagogue, the point is that the door that matters is not theirs to shut.\f*
\v 8 I know your works. Look, I have given before you an opened door, which no one is able to shut; because you have a little power\add [i.e. you are small and of no account here]\add*, and you kept my word and did not deny my name.
\v 9 Look, I am giving some out of the synagogue of Satan, those who say they are Jews and are not, but are lying — look, I will make them come and bow down before your feet, and they will know that I loved you.\f + \fr 3:9 \ft Isaiah promised Zion that "the sons of those who afflicted you will come bowing to you" (Isaiah 60:14), and Malachi opens with God's word to Israel, "I have loved you" (Malachi 1:2). Both are turned around and handed to this small assembly.\f*
\v 10 Because you kept the word of my endurance, I also will keep you out of the hour of testing that is about to come upon the whole inhabited world, to test those who dwell on the earth.\f + \fr 3:10 \ft The same construction — kept out of — is used in Jesus' prayer, "I do not ask you to take them out of the world, but to keep them from the evil one" (John 17:15). It is protection through the trial, not extraction from it: the hour is coming on the whole inhabited world, and they are in it.\f*
\v 11 I am coming quickly. Hold fast what you have, so that no one takes your wreath.\add [i.e. it is already yours, and it can still be taken]\add*
\v 12 The one who conquers — I will make him a pillar in the sanctuary of my God, and he will never go outside again\f + \fr 3:12 \ft Philadelphia sat on a fault line. The great earthquake of AD 17 flattened it, and the aftershocks kept people camped outside the walls for years afterward, afraid to go back in. A pillar that never has to leave the building meant something particular there.\f*; and I will write on him the name of my God and the name of the city of my God, the new Jerusalem coming down out of heaven from my God, and my own new name.
\v 13 Let the one who has an ear hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies.
\s1 To Laodicea: Rich, and Not Knowing What You Are
\p
\v 14 "And to the angel of the assembly in Laodicea write: These things says the Amen\f + \fr 3:14 \ft The Hebrew word of affirmation — surely, it is so — used here as a personal name. Isaiah 65:16 calls God "the God of Amen." The assembly that has its own condition entirely wrong is addressed by the one who is reliability itself.\f*, the faithful and true witness, the beginning of the creation of God:
\v 15 I know your works — that you are neither cold nor hot. I wish you were cold or hot.\f + \fr 3:15 \ft A local joke with a sting. Hierapolis, visible across the valley, had hot springs that people came to for their ailments; Colossae had cold, sweet drinking water. Laodicea's water arrived through a six-mile aqueduct, tepid and thick with minerals — the pipes are still furred with it. Cold water is good and hot water is good. Laodicea's water made you spit.\f*
\v 16 So, because you are lukewarm and neither hot nor cold, I am about to vomit you out of my mouth.\f + \fr 3:16 \ft "About to" — it has not happened. Leviticus warns that the land itself will vomit out inhabitants who defile it (Leviticus 18:28); here it is the Lord's own mouth, and verse 19 shows why he is still speaking.\f*
\v 17 Because you say, 'I am rich, and I have grown rich, and I have need of nothing,'\f + \fr 3:17 \ft The city's own boast. Laodicea was a banking centre, and when the earthquake of AD 60 levelled it the city refused imperial relief and rebuilt out of its own pocket. Hosea puts the same words in Ephraim's mouth: "Surely I have become rich, I have found wealth for myself" (Hosea 12:8).\f* and you do not know that you are the wretched one and pitiable and poor and blind and naked,
\v 18 I counsel you to buy from me gold fired in the fire, so that you may be rich; and white garments, so that you may be clothed and the shame of your nakedness not be shown; and eye-salve to smear on your eyes, so that you may see.\f + \fr 3:18 \ft Every item is one of the city's exports turned against it. Laodicea banked gold, wove a famous glossy black wool, and its medical school produced an eye ointment sold across the empire. The town that treats the world's eyes cannot see itself.\f*
\v 19 As many as I love, I reprove and discipline. Be zealous, then, and turn back.\f + \fr 3:19 \ft Proverbs 3:11-12: "whom the LORD loves he reproves, as a father the son in whom he delights." The harshest of the seven letters is also the only one that stops to say why it is harsh.\f*
\v 20 Look, I have taken my stand at the door and I am knocking. If anyone hears my voice and opens the door, I will come in to him and eat with him, and he with me.\f + \fr 3:20 \ft He held the key that no one could shut (3:7); here he stands outside knocking and waits. And the address narrows: not "you" the assembly but "anyone." Even in a body that is collectively lukewarm, one person can open a door. To share a meal in that world was to be family.\f*
\v 21 The one who conquers — I will give him to sit with me on my throne, as I also conquered and sat with my Father on his throne.
\v 22 Let the one who has an ear hear what the Spirit says to the assemblies."

\c 4
\s1 The Throne at the Center of Everything
\p
\v 1 After these things I looked, and there was an opened door in heaven, and the first voice that I heard, like a trumpet speaking with me, said, "Come up here, and I will show you what must happen after these things."\f + \fr 4:1 \ft "Come up here" was said to Moses at Sinai (Exodus 24:12), and everything in this room will be built out of Sinai and the tabernacle. The voice is the one from 1:10 — the same speaker who dictated the seven letters now opens the sky.\f*
\v 2 At once I came to be in spirit, and look — a throne standing in heaven, and on the throne one seated.\f + \fr 4:2 \ft The throne is the hinge of the whole book; the word occurs some forty-seven times in it, fourteen of them in this chapter alone. Everything else in the room is located by its position relative to this one thing. To assemblies who could see only Caesar's throne, the point is where the real one is.\f*
\v 3 And the one seated was like jasper stone and carnelian to look at\f + \fr 4:3 \ft No face, no form — only light through gemstones. Ezekiel does the same, describing not God but the effect of God (Ezekiel 1:26-28). In the Greek scriptures carnelian is the first stone of the high priest's breastpiece and jasper the last (Exodus 28:17-20), each row engraved with the names of the tribes. God is described in the colours of the whole covenant people, first to last.\f*, and around the throne a rainbow like an emerald to look at.\f + \fr 4:3 \ft The bow God set in the cloud as the sign of his promise never again to destroy all flesh (Genesis 9:12-17). It is in place before a single seal is broken. Whatever judgments follow, they unfold inside that promise.\f*
\v 4 And around the throne twenty-four thrones, and on the thrones twenty-four elders seated, clothed in white garments, and on their heads golden wreaths.\f + \fr 4:4 \ft David divided the priesthood into twenty-four courses that served in rotation, so that the full set covered the whole of the temple's ministry (1 Chronicles 24:1-19). Some also hear twelve patriarchs plus twelve sent ones, as in the gates and foundations of the city in 21:12-14. Either way the number stands for a complete body. Note what they wear: the white garments and the wreath that the assemblies are still being told to hold on to.\f*
\v 5 And out of the throne come lightnings and voices and thunders\f + \fr 4:5 \ft Sinai, still going on. "There were thunders and lightnings and a thick cloud upon the mountain" (Exodus 19:16). What Israel met once at the foot of a mountain pours continuously out of this throne, and the same formula returns at each climax of the book (8:5; 11:19; 16:18), a further element added each time.\f*; and seven torches of fire burning before the throne, which are the seven spirits of God;\f + \fr 4:5 \ft The menorah, in its place before the inner sanctuary — and Zechariah's seven lamps, which are "the eyes of the LORD ranging through the whole earth" (Zechariah 4:2, 10).\f*
\v 6 and before the throne something like a sea of glass, like crystal.\f + \fr 4:6 \ft Under God's feet at Sinai there appeared "a pavement of sapphire, as clear as the sky itself" (Exodus 24:10); over the living creatures Ezekiel saw an expanse "like the awesome gleam of crystal" (Ezekiel 1:22); and before Solomon's temple stood the great bronze basin simply called the Sea (1 Kings 7:23-26). Water for washing has become solid, still, and clear. In 15:2 the conquerors will stand on it.\f* And in the middle of the throne and around the throne, four living creatures full of eyes in front and behind.
\v 7 And the first living creature like a lion, and the second living creature like a calf, and the third living creature having a face like a man's, and the fourth living creature like a flying eagle.\f + \fr 4:7 \ft Ezekiel's cherubim had all four faces each (Ezekiel 1:10); John gives one face to each creature. They were read as the noblest of each order of living things — wild beast, working beast, bird, and man — the whole of creation drawn up around its maker.\f*
\v 8 And the four living creatures, each one of them having six wings\f + \fr 4:8 \ft Six wings and the threefold "holy" belong not to Ezekiel's cherubim but to Isaiah's seraphim (Isaiah 6:2-3). John fuses the two visions: what Isaiah saw in the temple and Ezekiel saw by the river are one room.\f*, are full of eyes around and within; and day and night they have no rest, saying, "Holy, holy, holy is the Lord God of hosts\add [i.e. holy beyond all comparison — in Hebrew you say a thing three times to put it past argument]\add*, the One who was and who is and who is coming."
\v 9 And whenever the living creatures give glory and honor and thanks to the one seated on the throne, the one living into the ages of the ages,
\v 10 the twenty-four elders will fall before the one seated on the throne and bow down to the one living into the ages of the ages, and they will throw their wreaths before the throne\f + \fr 4:10 \ft A subject king laid his crown before the sovereign who had given it. The assemblies are being told to hold on to their crowns (2:10; 3:11); here is what a crown is finally for.\f*, saying,
\v 11 "You are worthy, our Lord and God\f + \fr 4:11 \ft Suetonius reports that Domitian required to be addressed as "our lord and our god" (\tl dominus et deus noster\tl*). The elders say the words — to someone else.\f*, to receive the glory and the honor and the power, because you created all things, and by your will they were and were created."\add [i.e. nothing exists that you did not decide should exist]\add*

\c 5
\s1 The Sealed Scroll and the Lamb Who Can Open It
\p
\v 1 And I saw on the right hand of the one seated on the throne a scroll written inside and on the back, sealed with seven seals.\f + \fr 5:1 \ft Ezekiel too was shown a scroll "written on the front and on the back" — and what was written on it was lamentation and mourning and woe (Ezekiel 2:9-10). Writing on both sides means it is full: nothing can be added. Daniel was told to seal his book "until the time of the end" (Daniel 12:4, 9). Roman wills were sealed by seven witnesses and could not be executed until opened by the proper authority.\f*
\v 2 And I saw a strong angel proclaiming in a great voice, "Who is worthy to open the scroll and to loose its seals?"
\v 3 And no one in heaven or on the earth or under the earth\add [i.e. not one being anywhere that exists]\add* was able to open the scroll or to look into it.
\v 4 And I wept much, because no one was found worthy to open the scroll or to look into it.\add [i.e. if this cannot be opened, nothing I have been promised can happen]\add*
\v 5 And one of the elders says to me, "Do not weep. Look, the Lion of the tribe of Judah, the Root of David, has conquered, so as to open the scroll and its seven seals."\f + \fr 5:5 \ft The two most royal titles Israel had. The lion of Judah is Jacob's blessing — "the scepter shall not depart from Judah" (Genesis 49:9-10); the root of David is Isaiah's shoot from the stump of Jesse (Isaiah 11:1, 10). A first-century hearer knows exactly what is about to walk into view.\f*
\v 6 And I saw in the middle of the throne and of the four living creatures, and in the middle of the elders, a Lamb standing as though slaughtered\f + \fr 5:6 \ft This is the turn on which the whole book runs. John is told "Lion" and looks up and sees a Lamb — and not a lamb in glory but one bearing the marks of the knife, still standing. The two pictures are one figure: the conquering he was told about was done by being killed. Every later scene of power in this book has to be read through this one.\f*, having seven horns and seven eyes, which are the seven spirits of God sent out into all the earth.\f + \fr 5:6 \ft Horns are strength throughout the Hebrew scriptures; seven of them is all the strength there is. The seven eyes are the seven spirits of 1:4 and Zechariah's "eyes of the LORD ranging through the whole earth" (Zechariah 4:10). A slaughtered lamb with total power and total sight is not an animal anyone has seen. It is a statement built out of parts.\f*
\v 7 And he came and took it out of the right hand of the one seated on the throne.\f + \fr 5:7 \ft Daniel's son of man "came up to the Ancient of Days and was presented before him, and to him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom" (Daniel 7:13-14). The same approach, the same handing over — but the one approaching is a lamb with its throat cut.\f*
\v 8 And when he took the scroll, the four living creatures and the twenty-four elders fell before the Lamb, each of them having a harp and golden bowls full of incense, which are the prayers of the holy ones.\f + \fr 5:8 \ft "Let my prayer be counted as incense before you" (Psalm 141:2). The prayers of the assemblies are not lost in the air; they are collected, held in gold, and brought into the throne room. In 8:3-5 the same bowls will be emptied, and fire from that altar thrown at the earth.\f*
\v 9 And they sing a new song\f + \fr 5:9 \ft In the Psalms a new song is always the answer to a new act of rescue: "Sing to the LORD a new song, for he has done marvellous things" (Psalm 98:1; also 33:3; 40:3; 96:1; 144:9).\f*, saying, "You are worthy to take the scroll and to open its seals, because you were slaughtered, and by your blood you bought for God men out of every tribe and tongue and people and nation,\f + \fr 5:9 \ft The fourfold list is Daniel's formula for the whole human race (Daniel 3:4; 7:14). It recurs seven times in this book. The Lamb's purchase is not confined to one nation — and the assemblies in Asia, seven small congregations under pressure, are told they belong to something the size of the world.\f*
\v 10 and you made them a kingdom and priests to our God, and they will reign on the earth."\f + \fr 5:10 \ft Exodus 19:6 again, as in 1:6 — but now the exodus pattern is run at full scale: bought by blood out of every nation, then made a kingdom of priests. Note where the reigning happens: on the earth. This book's hope is not escape from the world but its government.\f*
\v 11 And I looked, and I heard the voice of many angels around the throne and the living creatures and the elders, and their number was myriads of myriads and thousands of thousands,\f + \fr 5:11 \ft Daniel 7:10, of the court of the Ancient of Days: "a thousand thousands served him, and ten thousand times ten thousand stood before him." The worship widens in rings — the creatures and elders, then the angels, then in verse 13 everything that exists.\f*
\v 12 saying in a great voice, "Worthy is the Lamb who was slaughtered to receive the power and riches and wisdom and strength and honor and glory and blessing."\add [i.e. seven things — the whole of what can be given]\add*
\v 13 And every creature that is in heaven and on the earth and under the earth and on the sea, and everything in them, I heard saying, "To the one seated on the throne and to the Lamb be the blessing and the honor and the glory and the might into the ages of the ages."\f + \fr 5:13 \ft This book refuses worship twice over, sharply, when John offers it to an angel (19:10; 22:8-9). Here every creature in existence directs it to God and to the Lamb together, and nothing corrects them. The point is not argued; it is sung.\f*
\v 14 And the four living creatures said, "Amen," and the elders fell and bowed down.

\c 6
\s1 The First Four Seals: Four Riders Sent Out
\p
\v 1 And I saw when the Lamb opened one of the seven seals, and I heard one of the four living creatures saying, like a voice of thunder, "Come."\add [i.e. spoken to the rider, not to me]\add*
\v 2 And I looked, and there was a white horse, and the one sitting on it holding a bow; and a wreath was given to him, and he went out conquering and to conquer.\f + \fr 6:2 \ft Zechariah saw horses of several colours dispatched from God's presence to patrol the earth (Zechariah 1:8-11; 6:1-8). Whatever each rider stands for, the crucial grammar is "was given" — a passive with no named agent, which is this book's way of saying God without saying it. These riders are let out; they do not break out.\f*
\v 3 And when he opened the second seal, I heard the second living creature saying, "Come."
\v 4 And another horse went out, fiery red, and to the one sitting on it it was given to take peace from the earth, and that they should slaughter one another; and a great sword was given to him.
\v 5 And when he opened the third seal, I heard the third living creature saying, "Come." And I looked, and there was a black horse, and the one sitting on it holding a balance in his hand.\f + \fr 6:5 \ft Scales, because grain has to be weighed out. Among the covenant curses: "they shall deliver your bread back by weight, and you shall eat and not be satisfied" (Leviticus 26:26); "they shall eat bread by weight and with anxiety" (Ezekiel 4:16).\f*
\v 6 And I heard something like a voice in the middle of the four living creatures saying, "A quart of wheat for a denarius, and three quarts of barley for a denarius; and do not harm the oil and the wine."\f + \fr 6:6 \ft A denarius was a labourer's day wage, and a quart of wheat one man's daily ration. At these prices a day's work feeds the worker and no one else — inflation of eight or ten times over. Oil and wine, the produce of the landowner's olive groves and vineyards, are untouched. Even the famine falls unequally.\f*
\v 7 And when he opened the fourth seal, I heard the voice of the fourth living creature saying, "Come."
\v 8 And I looked, and there was a pale green horse, and the one sitting on it — his name was Death, and Hades was following with him. And authority was given to them over a fourth of the earth, to kill with sword and with famine and with death and by the beasts of the earth.\f + \fr 6:8 \ft Ezekiel 14:21, where God names them as his own: "my four sore judgments upon Jerusalem — sword and famine and wild beasts and pestilence." The four riders are that list. And the licence is measured: a fourth, not all. The trumpets will take a third (8:7-12); the bowls will take everything.\f*
\s1 The Fifth Seal: The Slaughtered Ask How Long
\p
\v 9 And when he opened the fifth seal, I saw under the altar the souls of those slaughtered because of the word of God and because of the testimony they held.\f + \fr 6:9 \ft The blood of a sacrifice was poured out at the base of the altar (Leviticus 4:7), and "the life of the flesh is in the blood" (Leviticus 17:11). Under the altar is precisely where the lives of the offerings go. Their word for slaughtered is the Lamb's own word from 5:6. They died his death.\f*
\v 10 And they cried out in a great voice, saying, "How long, Master, holy and true, until you judge and avenge our blood on those who dwell on the earth?"\f + \fr 6:10 \ft "How long, O LORD?" is the standing cry of the righteous under injustice (Psalm 13:1; 79:5; 94:3; Habakkuk 1:2), and behind it Abel's blood crying from the ground (Genesis 4:10). It is not doubt; it is an appeal to God's own character, and the book will answer it — "a little longer" here, "no more delay" at 10:6, "he has avenged" at 19:2.\f*
\v 11 And a white robe was given to each of them, and they were told to rest yet a little time, until their fellow slaves and their brothers who were about to be killed as they had been should be filled up as well.\f + \fr 6:11 \ft The answer is not comfortable and is not meant to be. More are going to die first, and there is a number. The book does not promise its readers that they will be spared; it promises that those who are not will be vindicated.\f*
\s1 The Sixth Seal: The Day of the LORD Arrives
\p
\v 12 And I saw when he opened the sixth seal, and a great shaking happened, and the sun became black like sackcloth of hair, and the whole moon became like blood,\f + \fr 6:12 \ft Joel 2:31: "the sun shall be turned to darkness and the moon to blood, before the great and terrible day of the LORD comes." Sackcloth of hair is the coarsest black mourning cloth there is. The sun has put on mourning.\f*
\v 13 and the stars of heaven fell to the earth as a fig tree throws down its late figs when it is shaken by a great wind,
\v 14 and heaven was parted like a scroll being rolled up, and every mountain and island was moved out of its place.\f + \fr 6:14 \ft Isaiah 34:4: "the heaven shall be rolled up as a scroll, and all the stars shall fall as leaves fall from the fig tree." Isaiah, Ezekiel, and Joel all use this collapsing-sky language for the fall of particular empires — Babylon, Egypt, Edom. It is the standard prophetic way of saying that a world order is ending.\f*
\v 15 And the kings of the earth and the great men and the commanders and the rich and the strong, and every slave and free man, hid themselves in the caves and in the rocks of the mountains,\add [i.e. seven ranks, top to bottom — the whole social order, and none of it any help]\add*
\v 16 and they say to the mountains and to the rocks, "Fall on us and hide us from the face of the one seated on the throne and from the anger of the Lamb,\f + \fr 6:16 \ft Hosea 10:8: "they shall say to the mountains, Cover us, and to the hills, Fall on us." And "the anger of the Lamb" is a deliberate collision of words. Lambs do not have wrath. The one they are fleeing is the one they killed.\f*
\v 17 because the great day of their anger has come, and who is able to stand?"\f + \fr 6:17 \ft Nahum 1:6 and Malachi 3:2 ask this question and expect the answer "no one." Here it is left hanging, and the next chapter answers it.\f*

\c 7
\s1 God Marks His Own Before the Blow Falls
\p
\v 1 After this I saw four angels standing at the four corners of the earth, holding fast the four winds of the earth, so that no wind should blow on the earth or on the sea or on any tree.\add [i.e. the destruction is loaded and being held back]\add*
\v 2 And I saw another angel coming up from the rising of the sun\f + \fr 7:2 \ft The east, from which God's glory returns to the temple (Ezekiel 43:2) and from which the sun of righteousness rises with healing (Malachi 4:2). This angel comes from the direction of rescue.\f*, holding the seal of the living God, and he cried out in a great voice to the four angels to whom it was given to harm the earth and the sea,
\v 3 saying, "Do not harm the earth or the sea or the trees until we have sealed the slaves of our God on their foreheads."\f + \fr 7:3 \ft Ezekiel 9:4-6: a man in linen is sent through Jerusalem to put a mark on the foreheads of those who grieve over what is being done in it, and the executioners who follow are told to touch no one who carries it. Marking first, then judgment — as with Noah's ark and the blood on the doorposts. In 9:4 this seal will do actual work: the locusts may harm only those who lack it. And in 13:16 the beast will counterfeit it.\f*
\v 4 And I heard the number of the sealed\f + \fr 7:4 \ft Note the verb. John *hears* a number, and then in verse 9 he *looks* — and sees something that cannot be numbered at all. He heard "Lion" and saw "Lamb" in chapter 5 by the same technique. What is announced in Israel's own categories turns out, when looked at, to be larger than the categories.\f*: a hundred forty-four thousand, sealed out of every tribe of the sons of Israel:
\v 5 out of the tribe of Judah twelve thousand sealed, out of the tribe of Reuben twelve thousand, out of the tribe of Gad twelve thousand,
\v 6 out of the tribe of Asher twelve thousand, out of the tribe of Naphtali twelve thousand, out of the tribe of Manasseh twelve thousand,
\v 7 out of the tribe of Simeon twelve thousand, out of the tribe of Levi twelve thousand, out of the tribe of Issachar twelve thousand,
\v 8 out of the tribe of Zebulun twelve thousand, out of the tribe of Joseph twelve thousand, out of the tribe of Benjamin twelve thousand sealed.\f + \fr 7:5-8 \ft The list is unlike any other in the scriptures. Judah heads it, not Reuben the firstborn — the Lion's tribe goes first. Dan is missing altogether, and Ephraim is replaced by Joseph; both were bywords for idolatry (Judges 18:30-31; Hosea 4:17). Levi is included, though he is left out of the land lists, because this is not a muster for taking territory. And every tribe returns exactly twelve thousand, which no real census ever did. The number is twelve times twelve times a thousand: the people of God complete, none missing, none over-counted.\f*
\s1 The Crowd No One Can Count
\p
\v 9 After these things I looked, and there was a great crowd that no one was able to number\f + \fr 7:9 \ft The promise to Abraham: offspring like the dust of the earth, like the stars, like the sand — "if one can count the dust of the earth, your offspring also can be counted" (Genesis 13:16; 15:5; 22:17).\f*, out of every nation and tribes and peoples and tongues, standing before the throne and before the Lamb, clothed in white robes, and palm branches in their hands.\f + \fr 7:9 \ft Palm branches belong to the feast of Booths, when Israel lived in shelters for seven days remembering the wilderness and rejoicing in the harvest (Leviticus 23:40). They were also waved at national deliverances (1 Maccabees 13:51). Verse 15 will complete the picture: God spreads his tent over them.\f*
\v 10 And they cry out in a great voice, saying, "The rescue belongs to our God who is seated on the throne, and to the Lamb."\add [i.e. they are standing — this is the answer to "who is able to stand?"]\add*
\v 11 And all the angels stood around the throne and the elders and the four living creatures, and they fell on their faces before the throne and bowed down to God,
\v 12 saying, "Amen. The blessing and the glory and the wisdom and the thanks and the honor and the power and the strength be to our God into the ages of the ages. Amen."
\v 13 And one of the elders answered, saying to me, "These clothed in the white robes — who are they, and where did they come from?"
\v 14 And I said to him, "My lord, you know." And he said to me, "These are the ones coming out of the great pressure\f + \fr 7:14 \ft The present tense — coming out — describes a stream, not a single graduating class. And the word is the same one John used of himself and his readers in 1:9 and of Smyrna in 2:9. The great pressure is not a stranger to them.\f*, and they washed their robes and made them white in the blood of the Lamb.\add [i.e. blood that whitens instead of staining — his death is what cleaned them, and they had to do the washing]\add*
\v 15 Because of this they are before the throne of God, and they serve him day and night in his sanctuary\f + \fr 7:15 \ft The word is the technical one for priestly service. Under the old order one man entered the innermost place one day a year; here the whole crowd serves in it continually.\f*, and the one seated on the throne will spread his tent over them.\f + \fr 7:15 \ft Literally, will tabernacle over them — God's own dwelling pitched as a canopy above them. Isaiah promised exactly this: over Zion "a booth for shade by day from the heat, and a refuge and shelter from the storm" (Isaiah 4:5-6). The wilderness booths of the feast are replaced by God himself.\f*
\v 16 They will not hunger any more, nor thirst any more, nor will the sun fall on them, nor any heat,
\v 17 because the Lamb in the middle of the throne will shepherd them and will lead them to springs of waters of life, and God will wipe away every tear from their eyes."\f + \fr 7:16-17 \ft Nearly word for word from Isaiah 49:10 — no hunger, no thirst, no scorching sun, led to springs of water — with the wiping of tears from Isaiah 25:8. Both promises were made to exiles coming home. And a lamb is now doing the shepherding, which is what God said he would do himself (Ezekiel 34:11-16).\f*

\c 8
\s1 The Seventh Seal: Silence, Prayer, and Fire Thrown Down
\p
\v 1 And when he opened the seventh seal, there was silence in heaven for about half an hour.\f + \fr 8:1 \ft After four chapters of unbroken noise — thunder, thousands of thousands, the crying out of the slaughtered — nothing. The prophets command exactly this before God acts: "Be silent before the Lord GOD, for the day of the LORD is near" (Zephaniah 1:7; also Habakkuk 2:20; Zechariah 2:13). In the temple the people stood praying in silence outside while the priest offered the incense within (compare Luke 1:9-10) — and incense is what comes next.\f*
\v 2 And I saw the seven angels who stand before God, and seven trumpets were given to them.\f + \fr 8:2 \ft "The seven," with the article — a known group. Raphael says of himself, "I am one of the seven holy angels who present the prayers of the saints and enter before the glory of the Holy One" (Tobit 12:15). Seven priests with seven trumpets brought down Jericho's walls (Joshua 6:4-20).\f*
\v 3 And another angel came and stood at the altar, holding a golden censer, and much incense was given to him, so that he might give it with the prayers of all the holy ones on the golden altar before the throne.\f + \fr 8:3 \ft The golden altar of incense stood immediately before the veil of the innermost place (Exodus 30:1-6). On the day of atonement the high priest carried in a censer of coals from the altar and a handful of incense (Leviticus 16:12-13). This angel is doing the high priest's work.\f*
\v 4 And the smoke of the incense went up with the prayers of the holy ones out of the angel's hand before God.
\v 5 And the angel took the censer and filled it from the fire of the altar and threw it to the earth; and there were thunders and voices and lightnings and a shaking.\f + \fr 8:5 \ft This is the theological centre of the trumpet judgments. The same censer carries the prayers up and the fire down. Ezekiel saw the same act: a man in linen told to fill his hands with coals from between the cherubim and scatter them over the city (Ezekiel 10:2). The "how long?" of 6:10 is not being ignored — what follows is the answer coming back down.\f*
\s1 The First Four Trumpets Strike the Created World
\p
\v 6 And the seven angels holding the seven trumpets made themselves ready to sound them.
\v 7 And the first sounded, and there came hail and fire mixed with blood, and it was thrown to the earth; and a third of the earth was burned up, and a third of the trees was burned up, and all green grass was burned up.\f + \fr 8:7 \ft Egypt's seventh plague: "the LORD sent thunder and hail, and fire ran down to the earth" (Exodus 9:23-25), with the blood of the first plague added. The whole trumpet sequence is the plagues on Egypt run at the scale of the world. Note the fraction: a third, up from the seals' fourth, and still not everything.\f*
\v 8 And the second angel sounded, and something like a great mountain burning with fire was thrown into the sea; and a third of the sea became blood,\f + \fr 8:8 \ft Jeremiah's word to Babylon: "Behold, I am against you, O destroying mountain... I will roll you down from the crags and make you a burnt mountain" (Jeremiah 51:25).\f*
\v 9 and a third of the creatures in the sea that have life died, and a third of the ships were ruined.
\v 10 And the third angel sounded, and a great star fell out of heaven, burning like a torch, and it fell on a third of the rivers and on the springs of waters.
\v 11 And the name of the star is called Wormwood; and a third of the waters became wormwood, and many of the men died from the waters, because they were made bitter.\f + \fr 8:11 \ft Wormwood is a bitter desert shrub, proverbial for the taste of grief. God's sentence on a people who had abandoned his law: "I will feed them with wormwood and give them poisoned water to drink" (Jeremiah 9:15; also 23:15). The waters are not embittered at random; the prophets had already said why.\f*
\v 12 And the fourth angel sounded, and a third of the sun was struck, and a third of the moon, and a third of the stars, so that a third of them was darkened, and the day did not shine for a third of it, and the night likewise.\f + \fr 8:12 \ft Egypt's ninth plague, darkness that could be felt (Exodus 10:21-23) — but a third of it, not all. Taken together the four trumpets walk backward through the days of creation, striking land and vegetation, sea and sea-life, fresh water, and the lights in the sky. Judgment here looks like creation being undone.\f*
\s1 An Eagle Announces Three Woes
\p
\v 13 And I looked, and I heard one eagle flying in midheaven, saying in a great voice, "Woe, woe, woe to those who dwell on the earth\f + \fr 8:13 \ft "Those who dwell on the earth" is this book's fixed term for humanity set against God — the ones whose blood-guilt the slaughtered cry out over (6:10), who gloat when the witnesses die (11:10), who worship the beast (13:8). It is a posture, not an address. The first four trumpets struck the world; the last three come for these people.\f*, from the trumpet voices still to come from the three angels who are about to sound!"

\c 9
\s1 The Fifth Trumpet: The Abyss Opened
\p
\v 1 And the fifth angel sounded, and I saw a star that had fallen out of heaven to the earth, and the key of the shaft of the abyss was given to him.\f + \fr 9:1 \ft A star that is given something and then opens something is a person, not an object; stars stand for heavenly beings (Job 38:7; Daniel 8:10). The abyss is the deep of Genesis 1:2, and in Jewish writing the holding place of rebel spirits — the demons beg not to be sent there (Luke 8:31). Whatever this figure is, he does not force the lock. He is handed the key.\f*
\v 2 And he opened the shaft of the abyss, and smoke went up out of the shaft like the smoke of a great furnace, and the sun and the air were darkened by the smoke of the shaft.\f + \fr 9:2 \ft Abraham looked toward Sodom the morning after and saw "the smoke of the land going up like the smoke of a furnace" (Genesis 19:28).\f*
\v 3 And out of the smoke locusts came out onto the earth, and authority was given to them like the authority the scorpions of the earth have.\f + \fr 9:3 \ft Joel describes a locust invasion as the day of the LORD and calls the swarm God's own army — "the LORD utters his voice before his army" (Joel 2:11, 25). John takes Joel's picture and turns it: locusts strip vegetation, and these are forbidden to touch it. A scorpion's sting is agony and rarely kills, which is exactly the commission given here.\f*
\v 4 And it was said to them that they should not harm the grass of the earth or any green thing or any tree, but only the men who do not have the seal of God on their foreheads.\add [i.e. the seal of chapter 7 now does its work]\add*
\v 5 And it was given to them not to kill them, but that they should be tormented five months; and their torment is like the torment of a scorpion when it strikes a man.\f + \fr 9:5 \ft Five months is the locust's season in the Mediterranean, roughly late spring to early autumn. The judgment is bounded twice over: it may not kill, and it has a stated end.\f*
\v 6 And in those days men will seek death and will never find it, and they will long to die, and death flees from them.\add [i.e. the one thing they want is the one thing withheld]\add*
\v 7 And the appearance of the locusts was like horses made ready for war, and on their heads something like wreaths of gold, and their faces like men's faces,\f + \fr 9:7 \ft Joel again: "their appearance is like the appearance of horses, and like war horses so they run" (Joel 2:4). "Something like" wreaths, and only "like" gold — the crowns are imitations. This army wears a counterfeit of authority.\f*
\v 8 and they had hair like women's hair, and their teeth were like lions' teeth,\f + \fr 9:8 \ft "Its teeth are the teeth of a lion" (Joel 1:6).\f*
\v 9 and they had breastplates like breastplates of iron, and the sound of their wings was like the sound of chariots of many horses running into war.
\v 10 And they have tails like scorpions, and stingers; and in their tails is their authority to harm men five months.
\v 11 They have over them as king the angel of the abyss; his name in Hebrew is Abaddon, and in Greek he has the name Apollyon.\f + \fr 9:11 \ft "The locusts have no king" (Proverbs 30:27) — so these are not locusts. \tl Abaddon\tl* is a Hebrew name for the place of destruction, paired with Sheol (Job 26:6; Proverbs 15:11); \tl Apollyōn\tl* is Greek for Destroyer. The Greek name also sits one letter away from Apollo, the god Augustus claimed as his patron, whose temple at Didyma lay just down the coast from these assemblies, and who in Homer shoots plague-arrows into an army. A hearer who caught it would have understood that the empire's divine sponsor rules the pit.\f*
\v 12 The one woe has gone. Look, two woes are still coming after these things.
\s1 The Sixth Trumpet: The Cavalry from the Euphrates
\p
\v 13 And the sixth angel sounded, and I heard one voice out of the four horns of the golden altar before God,\f + \fr 9:13 \ft The same altar where the prayers of the holy ones were offered in 8:3-5. The order to launch this judgment issues from the place where the prayers were laid.\f*
\v 14 saying to the sixth angel who held the trumpet, "Loose the four angels who are bound at the great river Euphrates."\f + \fr 9:14 \ft The Euphrates is the far boundary of the land promised to Abraham (Genesis 15:18) and the line beyond which Israel's destroyers always massed — Assyria, Babylon, Persia. For a first-century reader it was also Rome's eastern frontier, beyond which lay Parthia, the one enemy Rome had never beaten and whose cavalry had annihilated a Roman army at Carrhae. These four have been held, bound, at that line.\f*
\v 15 And the four angels were loosed, who had been made ready for the hour and day and month and year, so that they might kill a third of men.\add [i.e. not a season but an exact moment, fixed in advance]\add*
\v 16 And the number of the armies of the cavalry was two myriads of myriads; I heard their number.\f + \fr 9:16 \ft Two hundred million. The whole population of the Roman empire was perhaps sixty million. The figure is not a count; it is a way of saying that no one could face this.\f*
\v 17 And this is how I saw the horses in the vision, and those sitting on them: having breastplates of fiery red and hyacinth blue and sulfur yellow; and the heads of the horses were like heads of lions, and out of their mouths goes fire and smoke and sulfur.\add [i.e. the riders wear the colours of the three things that kill]\add*
\v 18 By these three plagues a third of men were killed — by the fire and the smoke and the sulfur going out of their mouths.\f + \fr 9:18 \ft Fire and sulfur is what fell on Sodom (Genesis 19:24), and it becomes the standard prophetic shorthand for God's destruction of the irredeemable (Psalm 11:6; Ezekiel 38:22).\f*
\v 19 For the authority of the horses is in their mouth and in their tails; for their tails are like serpents, having heads, and with them they do harm.
\v 20 And the rest of men, who were not killed by these plagues, did not turn back from the works of their hands\f + \fr 9:20 \ft "The works of their hands" is the prophets' standing phrase for idols — a man worships what he manufactured (Deuteronomy 4:28; Psalm 115:4; Isaiah 2:8; Jeremiah 1:16). This verse and the next are the point of the whole trumpet sequence, and they state it by its failure. The plagues were meant to turn people, and did not. It is Pharaoh's pattern exactly: blow after blow, and the heart hardens.\f*, so as to stop bowing down to the demons and the idols of gold and silver and bronze and stone and wood, which can neither see nor hear nor walk;\f + \fr 9:20 \ft The mockery of Psalm 115:5-7 — "they have eyes but do not see, ears but do not hear... feet but do not walk." The list of materials is Belshazzar's, on the night his kingdom fell (Daniel 5:4, 23).\f*
\v 21 and they did not turn back from their murders or from their sorceries or from their whoring or from their thefts.\add [i.e. what they worship and how they live are one thing, and neither has changed]\add*

\c 10
\s1 The Angel with the Little Scroll: No More Delay
\p
\v 1 And I saw another strong angel coming down out of heaven, wrapped in a cloud, and the rainbow over his head, and his face like the sun, and his feet like pillars of fire,\f + \fr 10:1 \ft Every item belongs elsewhere to God or to Christ: the cloud of the presence (Exodus 13:21), the rainbow around the throne (4:3; Ezekiel 1:28), the face like the sun (1:16), the pillar of fire that led Israel (Exodus 13:21). Angels in the scriptures wear the insignia of the one who sends them — the angel in the burning bush spoke God's words and was not God (Exodus 3:2-4). The regalia measures the weight of the errand.\f*
\v 2 and holding in his hand a little scroll opened.\f + \fr 10:2 \ft Opened, unlike the sealed scroll of 5:1. Its seals have been broken; its contents are now available to be taken, eaten, and preached.\f* And he set his right foot on the sea and his left on the earth,\add [i.e. claiming both — and it is out of the sea and out of the earth that the two beasts will come]\add*
\v 3 and he cried out in a great voice as a lion roars. And when he cried out, the seven thunders spoke their own voices.\f + \fr 10:3 \ft Amos ties the roar to the prophet's compulsion: "A lion has roared! Who will not fear? The Lord GOD has spoken! Who can do anything but prophesy?" (Amos 3:8). And "the seven thunders," with the article, as though known: Psalm 29 says "the voice of the LORD" seven times, each time in thunder.\f*
\v 4 And when the seven thunders spoke, I was about to write; and I heard a voice out of heaven saying, "Seal up what the seven thunders spoke, and do not write it."\f + \fr 10:4 \ft He heard it and understood it well enough to start writing, and is stopped. Inside an unsealed book (22:10) there is a sealed patch. The revelation is vast and it is not everything: God has counsel he does not hand over.\f*
\v 5 And the angel whom I saw standing on the sea and on the earth raised his right hand to heaven
\v 6 and swore by the one living into the ages of the ages, who created the heaven and what is in it, and the earth and what is in it, and the sea and what is in it: that there will be no more time\add [i.e. no more waiting]\add*,\f + \fr 10:6 \ft Daniel 12:7 is being replayed: the man in linen above the river raises his hand to heaven and swears by the one who lives forever — and swears that it will be "a time, times, and half a time" yet. Here the same oath is sworn to the opposite effect. The waiting is over. This is the answer to the "little longer" of 6:11.\f*
\v 7 but in the days of the voice of the seventh angel, when he is about to sound, the secret of God is finished, as he announced the good news to his slaves the prophets.\f + \fr 10:7 \ft Amos 3:7: "Surely the Lord GOD does nothing without revealing his secret to his servants the prophets." Note that the completion of this — judgment included — is called good news. God's reign arriving is the gospel, whatever it costs those who fought it.\f*
\s1 John Eats the Scroll and Is Sent Out Again
\p
\v 8 And the voice that I heard out of heaven was speaking with me again, saying, "Go, take the scroll that is opened in the hand of the angel standing on the sea and on the earth."
\v 9 And I went off to the angel, telling him to give me the little scroll. And he says to me, "Take it and eat it up; and it will make your belly bitter, but in your mouth it will be sweet as honey."\f + \fr 10:9 \ft Ezekiel's commissioning: "eat this scroll, and go, speak to the house of Israel" — and it was "sweet as honey in my mouth" (Ezekiel 2:8-3:3). The scroll he ate was written full of "lamentations and mourning and woe." A prophet does not carry his message in his hand; he swallows it.\f*
\v 10 And I took the little scroll out of the angel's hand and ate it up, and in my mouth it was sweet as honey; and when I had eaten it, my belly was made bitter.\add [i.e. God's word is sweet to be given; what this one says is bitter to hold]\add*
\v 11 And they say to me, "You must prophesy again over many peoples and nations and tongues and kings."\f + \fr 10:11 \ft The fourth term is usually "tribes"; here it is "kings." What John is now sent back to say will run straight at political power — the beasts of chapter 13, Babylon and her client rulers in 17-18, the kings gathered for war in 19.\f*

\c 11
\s1 Measure the Sanctuary; Leave the Court to the Nations
\p
\v 1 And a reed like a staff was given to me, and it was said, "Rise and measure the sanctuary of God and the altar and those bowing down in it.\f + \fr 11:1 \ft Measuring in the prophets marks out what God claims and protects: a man with a measuring reed surveys the restored temple through nine chapters of Ezekiel (Ezekiel 40-42), and a man with a line goes to measure Jerusalem in Zechariah 2:1-5, where God says he will be a wall of fire around her. Buildings can be measured; worshippers cannot — including them says that the people, not the premises, are what is being marked off.\f*
\v 2 And the court outside the sanctuary — leave it out and do not measure it, because it was given to the nations, and they will trample the holy city forty-two months.\f + \fr 11:2 \ft The outer court was where non-Jews could go; a barrier and its warning inscriptions kept them from the rest. Given to the nations — given, again the passive: they are permitted, and for a stated term. Forty-two months is three and a half years, Daniel's "time, times, and half a time" during which the saints are handed over (Daniel 7:25; 12:7). It is half of seven: long enough to be terrible, cut off short of completion. The same span runs through 11:3, 12:6, 12:14, and 13:5.\f*
\v 3 And I will give to my two witnesses, and they will prophesy a thousand two hundred sixty days, clothed in sackcloth."\f + \fr 11:3 \ft Two, because "on the evidence of two witnesses a matter shall be confirmed" (Deuteronomy 19:15) — their testimony meets God's own standard for a valid case. Sackcloth is the prophet's mourning dress: this is grieving testimony, not triumphant announcement. And 1,260 days is the same forty-two months of verse 2. While the nations trample, the witnesses speak.\f*
\v 4 These are the two olive trees and the two lampstands standing before the Lord of the earth.\f + \fr 11:4 \ft Zechariah saw a lampstand flanked by two olive trees and was told they were "the two anointed ones who stand by the Lord of the whole earth" (Zechariah 4:2-3, 14) — in his day the high priest and the governor, priesthood and crown together. John doubles the lampstand, and in this book lampstands are assemblies (1:20). The two witnesses are the witnessing community.\f*
\v 5 And if anyone wants to harm them, fire goes out of their mouth and devours their enemies; and if anyone should want to harm them, in this way he must be killed.\f + \fr 11:5 \ft God to Jeremiah: "I am making my words in your mouth fire, and this people wood, and it shall consume them" (Jeremiah 5:14). Also Elijah, who called fire down on the men sent to arrest him (2 Kings 1:10-12). The weapon is the word.\f*
\v 6 These have the authority to shut heaven, so that no rain falls in the days of their prophesying; and they have authority over the waters, to turn them into blood, and to strike the earth with every plague as often as they want.\f + \fr 11:6 \ft Elijah shut the sky, and the drought ran three and a half years (1 Kings 17:1; James 5:17) — the same span again. Turning water to blood and striking with plagues is Moses. The two witnesses carry the powers of the Law and the Prophets, the two figures who stand at either end of Israel's testimony (Malachi 4:4-5).\f*
\s1 The Beast Kills the Witnesses, and God Raises Them
\p
\v 7 And when they have finished their testimony\add [i.e. not before — he cannot touch them until their work is done]\add*, the beast coming up out of the abyss will make war with them and will conquer them and will kill them.\f + \fr 11:7 \ft The beast's first appearance in the book; he is described in full in chapter 13. Note the verb: he *conquers* them — the same word the seven letters use for what the faithful must do. The beast wins the way the world counts winning, and this book has already shown what that is worth (5:5-6).\f*
\v 8 And their corpse lies on the street of the great city, which is called, in the spirit, Sodom and Egypt, where also their Lord was crucified.\f + \fr 11:8 \ft Three markers. Sodom and Egypt are not places here but verdicts — Isaiah addressed Jerusalem's rulers as "you rulers of Sodom" (Isaiah 1:9-10), and Egypt is the house of slavery. "Where their Lord was crucified" fixes the city as Jerusalem. The city that kept the exodus feast has become Egypt. Leaving a body unburied was the last contempt (Deuteronomy 28:26; Psalm 79:2-3).\f*
\v 9 And some from the peoples and tribes and tongues and nations look at their corpse three and a half days, and they do not allow their corpses to be put into a tomb.
\v 10 And those who dwell on the earth rejoice over them and make merry, and they will send gifts to one another, because these two prophets tormented those who dwell on the earth.\f + \fr 11:10 \ft A festival, with presents — the mirror of Purim, where Israel celebrated deliverance by "sending portions to one another" (Esther 9:22). And the word for what the prophets did to them is the word used for the locusts' sting in 9:5. True speech is felt as torture by people committed to the lie it exposes; Ahab called Elijah "the troubler of Israel" (1 Kings 18:17).\f*
\v 11 And after the three and a half days a spirit of life from God entered into them, and they stood on their feet, and great fear fell on those watching them.\f + \fr 11:11 \ft Ezekiel's valley of bones, almost word for word: "the breath came into them, and they lived and stood on their feet, an exceedingly great army" (Ezekiel 37:10).\f*
\v 12 And they heard a great voice out of heaven saying to them, "Come up here." And they went up into heaven in the cloud, and their enemies watched them.\add [i.e. the men who would not bury them have to stand and watch]\add*
\v 13 And in that hour a great shaking happened, and a tenth of the city fell, and seven thousand names of men were killed in the shaking; and the rest became terrified and gave glory to the God of heaven.\f + \fr 11:13 \ft Set this beside 9:20-21, where plague after plague produced no repentance at all. Here, after testimony, death, and vindication, the survivors give God glory — the very response the trumpets failed to get. "Give glory to God" is the demand made of Achan when he was called to confess (Joshua 7:19). Whatever else the book says about judgment, it says here that what breaks through is faithful witness, not force.\f*
\v 14 The second woe has gone. Look, the third woe is coming quickly.
\s1 The Seventh Trumpet: The Kingdom Has Changed Hands
\p
\v 15 And the seventh angel sounded, and there were great voices in heaven, saying, "The kingdom of the world has become our Lord's and his Christ's, and he will reign into the ages of the ages."\f + \fr 11:15 \ft Psalm 2:2, where the kings of the earth take counsel "against the LORD and against his anointed" — and God laughs and installs his king anyway. "Has become" is stated as an accomplished fact, though on earth the beast has not yet even been described. Heaven is reporting the outcome, not the score.\f*
\v 16 And the twenty-four elders who sit on their thrones before God fell on their faces and bowed down to God,
\v 17 saying, "We thank you, Lord God of hosts, the One who is and who was\add [i.e. and no longer "who is coming" — he has come]\add*, because you have taken your great power and begun to reign.\f + \fr 11:17 \ft The divine title has been threefold all through the book — who is, who was, and who is coming (1:4, 8; 4:8). Here the third clause quietly drops off, and it stays off (16:5). The coming has happened.\f*
\v 18 And the nations were angered, and your anger came, and the time for the dead to be judged, and to give the reward to your slaves the prophets and to the holy ones and to those who fear your name, the small and the great, and to ruin those who ruin the earth."\add [i.e. the destroyers destroyed — the punishment is the crime handed back]\add*
\v 19 And the sanctuary of God in heaven was opened, and the ark of his covenant was seen in his sanctuary; and there were lightnings and voices and thunders and a shaking and great hail.\f + \fr 11:19 \ft The ark had been lost since Babylon burned the temple in 586 BC, and Israel had not seen it since. It held the tablets of the covenant and stood in the one room no one entered but the high priest, once a year. At the moment the kingdom is declared transferred, the chest that carries God's sworn word is put on view. Whatever is coming, it comes out of a covenant that was never abandoned.\f*

\c 12
\s1 The Woman, the Dragon, and the Son
\p
\v 1 And a great sign appeared in heaven: a woman clothed with the sun, and the moon underneath her feet, and on her head a wreath of twelve stars;\f + \fr 12:1 \ft Joseph's dream: "the sun and the moon and eleven stars were bowing down to me," which his father read as himself, Joseph's mother, and the brothers (Genesis 37:9-10). Here the figure is not one of the family but the family entire — Israel, the covenant people, with all twelve. And she wears a victor's wreath, not a royal diadem; the diadems in this chapter belong to the dragon.\f*
\v 2 and she is pregnant, and she cries out in labor, in agony to give birth.\f + \fr 12:2 \ft Zion in labour is a standing prophetic image. Isaiah's Zion writhes and produces nothing (Isaiah 26:17-18); his later Zion "before her pain came, was delivered of a male child" (Isaiah 66:7); Micah's daughter of Zion labours, and out of it comes the ruler from Bethlehem (Micah 4:9-10; 5:2-3). The Messiah is not born into Israel's ease but out of her anguish.\f*
\v 3 And another sign appeared in heaven, and look — a great fiery red dragon, having seven heads and ten horns, and on his heads seven diadems.\f + \fr 12:3 \ft The dragon is the chaos monster of Israel's poetry — Leviathan, Rahab, the sea beast God broke at creation and will slay at the end (Isaiah 27:1; 51:9; Psalm 74:13-14) — and the name Ezekiel gives Pharaoh (Ezekiel 29:3). The ten horns are Daniel's fourth beast (Daniel 7:7). Verse 9 will name him outright.\f*
\v 4 And his tail drags a third of the stars of heaven, and he threw them to the earth. And the dragon stood before the woman who was about to give birth, so that when she gave birth he might devour her child.\f + \fr 12:4 \ft The posture is the oldest strategy against God's purposes: Pharaoh ordering the Hebrew boys killed (Exodus 1:15-22), Herod ordering the children of Bethlehem killed (Matthew 2:16). Kill the deliverer before he can grow.\f*
\v 5 And she bore a son, a male, who is about to shepherd all the nations with an iron staff; and her child was snatched up to God and to his throne.\f + \fr 12:5 \ft Psalm 2:9, the coronation psalm, identifies the child beyond mistake. Between his birth and his enthronement the vision says nothing at all — no ministry, no cross, no tomb. A sign gives the bare shape: he was born of Israel, the dragon tried to kill him, and God has him on the throne.\f*
\v 6 And the woman fled into the wilderness, where she has a place made ready by God, so that they might feed her there a thousand two hundred sixty days.\f + \fr 12:6 \ft The wilderness is where Israel was fed with manna for forty years and where Elijah was fed by ravens and by an angel when Jezebel wanted him dead (Exodus 16; 1 Kings 17:4-6; 19:5-8). It is not exile but shelter, and the place was prepared in advance. The 1,260 days are the same three and a half years as 11:2-3 and 13:5.\f*
\s1 War in Heaven and the Accuser Thrown Down
\p
\v 7 And war happened in heaven: Michael and his angels making war with the dragon. And the dragon made war, and his angels,\f + \fr 12:7 \ft Michael is Israel's own angelic prince in Daniel, and Daniel 12:1 sets him precisely here: "at that time Michael, the great prince who stands guard over your people, will arise, and there will be a time of distress such as never was."\f*
\v 8 and he was not strong enough, nor was a place found for them any longer in heaven.\f + \fr 12:8 \ft He had a place there. In Job 1-2 and Zechariah 3:1-2 the accuser stands in the heavenly court and lodges charges against God's servants. That standing is what he has now lost.\f*
\v 9 And the great dragon was thrown down — the ancient serpent, the one called Devil and Satan, the one leading the whole inhabited world astray — he was thrown down to the earth, and his angels were thrown down with him.\f + \fr 12:9 \ft Four names, so there can be no doubt who this is. The dragon of the prophets; the serpent of the garden (Genesis 3:1-15), against whose seed God set the woman's seed; \tl diabolos\tl*, Greek for slanderer; and \tl satan\tl*, Hebrew for adversary. His method is named too, and it is not force: he leads astray. Deception is his weapon throughout this book.\f*
\v 10 And I heard a great voice in heaven saying, "Now the rescue and the power and the kingdom of our God and the authority of his Christ have come, because the accuser of our brothers has been thrown down, the one accusing them before our God day and night.
\v 11 And they conquered him because of the blood of the Lamb and because of the word of their testimony, and they did not love their life all the way to death.\f + \fr 12:11 \ft This is the book's definition of conquering, and all seven promises to the overcomer are cashed here. Three things answer the accuser: the Lamb's blood answers the charge of guilt, their spoken testimony answers the charge of falsehood, and their willingness to die answers his one remaining threat. Once a man will not buy his life at the price of his testimony, the accuser has nothing left to work with.\f*
\v 12 For this, be glad, heavens, and you who tent in them. Woe to the earth and the sea, because the devil has come down to you with great fury, knowing that he has little time."\f + \fr 12:12 \ft The hinge of the whole book's pastoral logic. The persecution about to be described is not evidence that the dragon is winning; it is what a defeated and cornered enemy does with the time he has left. The worse it gets, the shorter his run.\f*
\s1 The Dragon Turns on the Woman's Other Children
\p
\v 13 And when the dragon saw that he had been thrown down to the earth, he pursued the woman who bore the male.
\v 14 And the two wings of the great eagle were given to the woman, so that she might fly into the wilderness to her place, where she is fed there a time and times and half a time, away from the face of the serpent.\f + \fr 12:14 \ft "I bore you on eagles' wings and brought you to myself," God said of the exodus (Exodus 19:4; also Deuteronomy 32:11). "A time and times and half a time" is Daniel's own phrase (Daniel 7:25; 12:7) for the same three and a half years given as 1,260 days in verse 6 — the author says it both ways so the link to Daniel cannot be missed.\f*
\v 15 And the serpent threw water out of his mouth like a river after the woman, so that he might have her carried off by the river.\f + \fr 12:15 \ft Overwhelming waters are the psalms' picture of enemies about to swallow a man whole: "then the waters would have engulfed us, the torrent would have gone over our soul" (Psalm 124:4-5). What comes out of this mouth may be a flood of lies as much as of force; deception is what he does.\f*
\v 16 And the earth helped the woman, and the earth opened its mouth and swallowed the river that the dragon threw out of his mouth.\f + \fr 12:16 \ft The earth opened its mouth and swallowed Korah and his company when they rebelled (Numbers 16:31-33). Here the same act rescues. The dragon has been thrown down to the earth, but the earth is not his: it works against him.\f*
\v 17 And the dragon was angered at the woman, and he went off to make war with the rest of her seed, those who keep the commandments of God and hold the testimony of Jesus.\f + \fr 12:17 \ft "Her seed" is the word of Genesis 3:15 — "I will put enmity between you and the woman, and between your seed and her seed." This whole chapter has been that sentence unfolded. The rest of her seed are the assemblies reading this letter, and the two marks named here — obedience and confession — are what identifies them right through to 14:12.\f*
\v 18 And he stood on the sand of the sea.\add [i.e. at the water's edge, waiting for what is about to come up out of it]\add*

\c 13
\s1 The Beast Out of the Sea
\p
\v 1 And I saw coming up out of the sea a beast having ten horns and seven heads, and on his horns ten diadems, and on his heads names of blasphemy.\f + \fr 13:1 \ft Daniel saw four beasts come up out of the sea, and was told they were four kingdoms (Daniel 7:2-3, 17). John's single beast has the features of all four at once: it is not one empire but empire itself. The blasphemous names are the divine titles an emperor took — \tl sebastos\tl* (revered), \tl sōtēr\tl* (saviour), \tl kyrios\tl* (lord), son of the deified. To a people who confessed one God, those words on a man were blasphemy written across his face.\f*
\v 2 And the beast that I saw was like a leopard, and his feet like a bear's, and his mouth like a lion's mouth. And the dragon gave him his power and his throne and great authority.\f + \fr 13:2 \ft Daniel's lion, bear, and leopard (Daniel 7:4-6), run backward and fused. And note the transfer: power, throne, and authority handed down from dragon to beast. It is a counterfeit of what God does with the Lamb — a parody, deliberately built.\f*
\v 3 And one of his heads was as though slaughtered to death, and his death-stroke was healed. And the whole earth marveled after the beast,\f + \fr 13:3 \ft "As though slaughtered" is the exact phrase used of the Lamb in 5:6. The beast copies the one thing that made the Lamb worthy — death survived — and the world follows it for that reason. Behind it a first-century reader would have heard the story that Nero, who killed himself in 68 and whose death threw the empire into civil war, was not really dead and would come back. Whether or not John expected that literally, the imitation is the point: the beast's credential is a forged resurrection.\f*
\v 4 and they bowed down to the dragon, because he gave the authority to the beast; and they bowed down to the beast, saying, "Who is like the beast, and who is able to make war with him?"\f + \fr 13:4 \ft "Who is like you, O LORD?" is Israel's oldest song of praise, sung at the sea (Exodus 15:11; also Psalm 35:10; 89:8; Micah 7:18) — and the name Michael means "who is like God?" The words are lifted whole and set on a creature. That is what idolatry is, in one sentence.\f*
\v 5 And a mouth was given to him speaking great things and blasphemies, and authority was given to him to act forty-two months.\f + \fr 13:5 \ft "A mouth speaking great things" is Daniel 7:8, of the little horn. And twice in one verse: was given. The beast's mouth and the beast's term are both issued to him. Forty-two months is the same bounded span as 11:2-3 and 12:6 — the assemblies are told the expiry date, and the beast is not.\f*
\v 6 And he opened his mouth in blasphemies against God, to blaspheme his name and his tent — those who tent in heaven.\add [i.e. his dwelling, that is to say the people who dwell in it]\add*
\v 7 And it was given to him to make war with the holy ones and to conquer them; and authority was given to him over every tribe and people and tongue and nation.\f + \fr 13:7 \ft Daniel 7:21, 25: the horn "was waging war with the saints and overpowering them," and they were "given into his hand for a time, times, and half a time." This is a hard sentence and the book does not soften it. The beast really does defeat them — imprisons, impoverishes, kills them. And 12:11 has already said what that defeat actually is.\f*
\v 8 And all who dwell on the earth will bow down to him — everyone whose name has not been written in the scroll of life of the Lamb slaughtered from the founding of the world.\add [i.e. all of them — except those in the book, and their names were entered before any of this began]\add*
\v 9 If anyone has an ear, let him hear.\add [i.e. the same call that closed each of the seven letters — this is addressed to you]\add*
\v 10 If anyone is for captivity, into captivity he goes. If anyone is to be killed by the sword, by the sword he is to be killed.\f + \fr 13:10 \ft Jeremiah 15:2, on the inevitability of an appointed judgment: "those for death, to death; those for the sword, to the sword; those for captivity, to captivity." Read one way it tells the faithful that what is coming cannot be dodged and must be endured. Read the other way it warns the persecutors that what they deal out is what they will get. Both readings are at work; the sentence that follows shows which one the assemblies are to act on.\f* Here is the endurance and the trust of the holy ones.\add [i.e. this is the point at which endurance and trust are needed, and this is what they look like]\add*
\s1 The Beast Out of the Earth and the Mark
\p
\v 11 And I saw another beast coming up out of the earth, and he had two horns like a lamb, and he spoke like a dragon.\f + \fr 13:11 \ft Called "the false prophet" from 16:13 onward. If the first beast is imperial power arriving from over the sea, this one rises out of the local ground — the provincial councils, the priesthoods of the imperial cult, the city magistrates who actually enforced loyalty in Asia. Looks like a lamb, speaks like a dragon: the whole of false prophecy in one line (compare Matthew 7:15).\f*
\v 12 And he exercises all the authority of the first beast in his presence, and he makes the earth and those dwelling in it bow down to the first beast, whose death-stroke was healed.
\v 13 And he does great signs, so that he even makes fire come down out of heaven to the earth before men.\f + \fr 13:13 \ft Elijah's sign on Carmel, by which Israel knew who the true God was (1 Kings 18:36-39), and the sign of the two witnesses in 11:5. It is being counterfeited. The Torah had already said that a sign can come true and the prophet still be a liar — the test is not the wonder but where it sends you (Deuteronomy 13:1-3).\f*
\v 14 And he leads astray those who dwell on the earth, because of the signs it was given him to do in the presence of the beast, telling those who dwell on the earth to make an image to the beast who has the stroke of the sword and came to life.
\v 15 And it was given to him to give spirit to the image of the beast, so that the image of the beast might even speak, and might make as many as would not bow down to the image of the beast be killed.\f + \fr 13:15 \ft This is Daniel 3 happening again: an image set up, all peoples and tongues commanded to fall down before it, and death for anyone who will not (Daniel 3:1-6). The prophets mocked idols precisely because they cannot speak and have no breath in them (Psalm 115:5-7; Habakkuk 2:18-19). This one is given breath and speech — which does not make it a god, only a better lie.\f*
\v 16 And he makes all — the small and the great, and the rich and the poor, and the free and the slaves — to be given a mark on their right hand or on their forehead,\f + \fr 13:16 \ft Greek \tl charagma\tl*, the stamp struck on a coin or branded on a slave: the mark of who owns you. Its placement is a direct inversion of Israel's practice — "bind them as a sign on your hand, and let them be as frontlets before your eyes" (Deuteronomy 6:8). Where God's people wore his words on hand and forehead, the beast's wear his brand. Everyone in this book is marked one way or the other; there is no third option.\f*
\v 17 so that no one is able to buy or to sell except the one having the mark: the name of the beast or the number of his name.\f + \fr 13:17 \ft Before the sword comes the stomach. In the cities of Asia the trade guilds carried patron gods and honours to the emperor; a man who would not join in lost the guild and the trade with it. Refusal was not a gesture — it was destitution.\f*
\v 18 Here is wisdom. Let the one who has understanding count the number of the beast, for it is a man's number; and his number is six hundred sixty-six.\f + \fr 13:18 \ft Letters doubled as numerals, and adding up the letters of a name was a common game — a wall at Pompeii carries "I love her whose number is 545." "Nero Caesar" written in Hebrew letters totals 666; drop the final letter, as the Latin spelling does, and it totals 616 — which is precisely the variant reading some manuscripts carry. Both roads lead to the same man. And there is a second thing in the number itself: seven is completeness in this book, and six is what falls short of it. Three sixes is a creature straining to be God and missing, three times over.\f*

\c 14
\s1 The Lamb and the Hundred Forty-Four Thousand on Zion
\p
\v 1 And I looked, and there was the Lamb standing on Mount Zion\f + \fr 14:1 \ft "I have installed my king upon Zion, my holy mountain" (Psalm 2:6), and "on Mount Zion there will be those who escape" (Joel 2:32; Obadiah 17). The beast was last seen standing on the sand at the water's edge (12:18). The Lamb stands on the mountain God chose.\f*, and with him a hundred forty-four thousand having his name and the name of his Father written on their foreheads.\add [i.e. these are the sealed of chapter 7, and this is what the seal says]\add*
\v 2 And I heard a voice out of heaven like the sound of many waters and like the sound of great thunder; and the voice that I heard was like harpists harping on their harps.
\v 3 And they sing a new song before the throne and before the four living creatures and the elders; and no one was able to learn the song except the hundred forty-four thousand, the ones bought from the earth.\add [i.e. only those who lived it can sing it]\add*
\v 4 These are the ones who were not defiled with women, for they are virgins.\f + \fr 14:4 \ft Read through the prophets, not through a monastery. Unfaithfulness to God is described as whoring all through Hosea, Jeremiah, and Ezekiel, and in this book the great whore is Babylon, with whom the kings of the earth commit adultery (17:2). These are men who did not go to bed with her system. The same word for defilement is used at Sardis of garments, not of bodies (3:4). There is a second note under it: soldiers on a holy campaign kept themselves from women (1 Samuel 21:5; 2 Samuel 11:11), and this hundred forty-four thousand was mustered like an army (7:4-8).\f* These are the ones following the Lamb wherever he goes.\add [i.e. and he went to death]\add* These were bought from among men as firstfruits to God and to the Lamb,\f + \fr 14:4 \ft The first sheaf of the harvest, brought to the priest and consecrated before the rest could be gathered in (Leviticus 23:10-14). Firstfruits are not the whole crop; they are the pledge that the crop is coming.\f*
\v 5 and in their mouth no lie was found; they are without blemish.\f + \fr 14:5 \ft Zephaniah 3:13 of the surviving remnant: "no deceitful tongue will be found in their mouths." Against a dragon whose trade is deception, a community in whose mouth there is no lie is itself a form of resistance. "Without blemish" is the word for a sacrificial animal fit to be offered (Leviticus 1:3) — which is what firstfruits have to be.\f*
\s1 Three Angels: Good News, Babylon's Fall, and a Warning
\p
\v 6 And I saw another angel flying in midheaven, having good news of the age to announce to those seated on the earth, and to every nation and tribe and tongue and people,\f + \fr 14:6 \ft The only occurrence of the word "gospel" in the book, and its content is given in the next verse: fear God, give him glory, worship the maker. Isaiah's herald says the same thing in three words — "your God reigns" (Isaiah 52:7). Even here, this late, the announcement goes out to everyone.\f*
\v 7 saying in a great voice, "Fear God and give him glory, because the hour of his judging has come; and bow down to the one who made the heaven and the earth and the sea and springs of waters."\f + \fr 14:7 \ft Exodus 20:11, the ground of the sabbath command: God made heaven, earth, sea, and everything in them. The beast demands worship as a creature; the answer is to name the Creator. That is the whole argument.\f*
\v 8 And another, a second angel, followed, saying, "Fallen, fallen is Babylon the great, who has given all the nations to drink of the wine of the fury of her whoring."\f + \fr 14:8 \ft Isaiah 21:9, the cry that announced the fall of the empire that burned the temple — spoken in the past tense of something not yet done, because what God has settled is as good as finished. Jeremiah calls Babylon "a golden cup in the LORD's hand, making all the earth drunk" (Jeremiah 51:7). The name is not explained here; chapters 17-18 will do that.\f*
\v 9 And another, a third angel, followed them, saying in a great voice, "If anyone bows down to the beast and his image and receives a mark on his forehead or on his hand,
\v 10 he also will drink of the wine of the fury of God, poured out undiluted into the cup of his anger\f + \fr 14:10 \ft The cup of God's wrath is a fixed prophetic image (Psalm 75:8; Isaiah 51:17; Jeremiah 25:15-16). Wine was ordinarily mixed with water before drinking; this cup is prepared and not watered down. Nothing has been added to soften it.\f*; and he will be tormented in fire and sulfur before the holy angels and before the Lamb.
\v 11 And the smoke of their torment goes up into the ages of the ages, and they have no rest day and night — those who bow down to the beast and his image, and whoever receives the mark of his name."\f + \fr 14:11 \ft Isaiah 34:9-10, of Edom: pitch and sulfur, a fire not quenched night or day, "its smoke shall go up forever." And set "no rest day and night" against verse 13, where the faithful dead do rest. That contrast is the choice the whole chapter is putting to the reader.\f*
\v 12 Here is the endurance of the holy ones, those who keep the commandments of God and the trust of Jesus.\add [i.e. knowing what the other road ends in is what makes it possible to stay on this one]\add*
\v 13 And I heard a voice out of heaven saying, "Write: Blessed are the dead who die in the Lord from now on. Yes, says the Spirit, so that they may rest from their labors, for their works follow with them."\f + \fr 14:13 \ft To people facing execution for refusing the mark, heaven's verdict on that death is: blessed. "From now on" is deliberate — under this pressure, at this moment. And their works go with them; nothing done in faithfulness is left behind in the grave (compare 20:12).\f*
\s1 Two Harvests
\p
\v 14 And I looked, and there was a white cloud, and on the cloud one seated like a son of man, having on his head a golden wreath and in his hand a sharp sickle.\f + \fr 14:14 \ft Daniel 7:13 again — the figure of 1:13, now come to reap. Both harvest scenes are drawn from a single verse of Joel: "Put in the sickle, for the harvest is ripe; come, tread, for the winepress is full" (Joel 3:13). John separates the grain from the grapes.\f*
\v 15 And another angel came out of the sanctuary, crying out in a great voice to the one seated on the cloud, "Send your sickle and reap, because the hour to reap has come, because the harvest of the earth is dried out."\add [i.e. the grain has stood until it is ready, and the moment cannot be put off]\add*
\v 16 And the one seated on the cloud swung his sickle over the earth, and the earth was reaped.\f + \fr 14:16 \ft Nothing is said here about wrath, and the reaper is the son of man himself; the vintage that follows says everything about wrath, and an angel does it. The hundred forty-four thousand were called firstfruits (14:4), which implies a full harvest still to be gathered. The text does not spell out the difference, but it takes care to draw it.\f*
\v 17 And another angel came out of the sanctuary in heaven, and he also had a sharp sickle.
\v 18 And another angel came out from the altar, the one having authority over the fire, and he called in a great voice to the one having the sharp sickle, saying, "Send your sharp sickle and gather the clusters of the vine of the earth, because its grapes are ripe."\f + \fr 14:18 \ft From the altar — the place where the slaughtered lie and their prayers were offered (6:9; 8:3-5). The order for this vintage comes from under the altar. It is the answer to "how long?"\f*
\v 19 And the angel swung his sickle to the earth and gathered the vine of the earth, and he threw it into the great winepress of the fury of God.
\v 20 And the winepress was trodden outside the city, and blood came out of the winepress up to the bridles of the horses, for a thousand six hundred stadia.\f + \fr 14:20 \ft Isaiah 63:1-6: "I have trodden the winepress alone... their lifeblood spattered my garments." The grapes are men, and the juice is blood. Sixteen hundred stadia is roughly a hundred and eighty miles — four squared times a hundred, four being this book's number for the earth's four corners. It is not a distance to be measured but a way of saying that nowhere is out of reach. In 19:15 the rider will tread this press himself.\f*

\c 15
\s1 Those Who Conquered the Beast Sing on the Far Shore
\p
\v 1 And I saw another sign in heaven, great and marvelous: seven angels having seven plagues, the last ones, because in them the fury of God is finished.\f + \fr 15:1 \ft The third great sign, after the woman and the dragon (12:1, 3). "The last ones" — after these there are no more. God's wrath is not an endless condition but a finite act with a stated end, and this is it.\f*
\v 2 And I saw something like a sea of glass mixed with fire, and those conquering from the beast and from his image and from the number of his name, standing on the sea of glass, holding harps of God.\f + \fr 15:2 \ft The still sea before the throne (4:6), now shot through with fire, and people standing on it. This is Israel on the far bank of the Red Sea, watching the water close over Egypt. The three things they conquered — the beast, his image, his number — are exactly the three coercions of chapter 13: worship, the statue, and the mark required for trade. They faced all three and refused all three. In 13:7 the beast conquered them. Here they are, standing.\f*
\v 3 And they sing the song of Moses the slave of God and the song of the Lamb, saying, "Great and marvelous are your works, Lord God of hosts. Righteous and true are your ways, King of the nations.\f + \fr 15:3 \ft One song with two names. Moses sang on the shore after the sea closed (Exodus 15), and sang again at the end of his life about God's just ways and his vindication of his servants (Deuteronomy 32). The words here are woven out of that second song and the psalms: "all his ways are just" (Deuteronomy 32:4), "great are the works of the LORD" (Psalm 111:2). The Lamb's deliverance is the exodus done again and done finally, so the song is the same song.\f*
\v 4 Who will not fear, Lord, and glorify your name? Because you alone are holy. Because all the nations will come and bow down before you, because your righteous acts were made visible."\f + \fr 15:4 \ft "Who would not fear you, O King of the nations?" (Jeremiah 10:7), and "all nations whom you have made will come and worship before you" (Psalm 86:9). Before a single bowl is poured, the book tells you how to read what follows: these are righteous acts, and the end of them is the nations worshipping.\f*
\s1 Seven Angels Receive the Bowls
\p
\v 5 And after these things I looked, and the sanctuary of the tent of the testimony in heaven was opened,\f + \fr 15:5 \ft "The tent of the testimony" is the Greek scriptures' name for the wilderness tabernacle (Numbers 1:50), so called because it housed the tablets of the covenant — God's testimony, his sworn word about who he is and what he requires. What comes out of that room is not arbitrary power. It is covenant consequence.\f*
\v 6 and the seven angels having the seven plagues came out of the sanctuary, clothed in clean bright linen and belted around the chest with golden belts.\f + \fr 15:6 \ft Linen and a golden belt across the chest is how the risen Christ was dressed in 1:13, and linen is the high priest's garment for the day of atonement (Leviticus 16:4). These are priests, not soldiers, and they are dressed like the one who sent them.\f*
\v 7 And one of the four living creatures gave to the seven angels seven golden bowls full of the fury of God, the one living into the ages of the ages.\f + \fr 15:7 \ft The last golden bowls in this book held the prayers of the holy ones (5:8). The same vessel now carries the answer back down.\f*
\v 8 And the sanctuary was filled with smoke from the glory of God and from his power, and no one was able to enter into the sanctuary until the seven plagues of the seven angels were finished.\f + \fr 15:8 \ft When the tabernacle was finished, "Moses was not able to enter, because the cloud had settled on it and the glory of the LORD filled it" (Exodus 40:34-35), and at the temple's dedication the priests "could not stand to minister because of the cloud" (1 Kings 8:10-11). No one goes in while this is happening — which is to say that the time for intercession has run out. Once the bowls start, they finish.\f*

\c 16
\s1 The Bowls Poured Out
\p
\v 1 And I heard a great voice out of the sanctuary saying to the seven angels, "Go and pour out the seven bowls of the fury of God into the earth."\add [i.e. and since no one could enter the sanctuary, the voice inside it is God's own]\add*
\v 2 And the first went off and poured out his bowl into the earth; and a bad and evil sore came on the men having the mark of the beast and bowing down to his image.\f + \fr 16:2 \ft Egypt's sixth plague, boils breaking out on man and beast — and on the magicians themselves, so that they could not stand before Moses (Exodus 9:8-11). Note who is struck: those carrying the mark. As God put a division between Goshen and Egypt (Exodus 8:22-23; 9:4), so here.\f*
\v 3 And the second poured out his bowl into the sea; and it became blood as of a dead man, and every living soul died — the things in the sea.\f + \fr 16:3 \ft Egypt's first plague, the Nile to blood and the fish dead (Exodus 7:20-21) — but not a third of the sea now, as at the second trumpet, and not the Nile but the sea. The measure has run out. "Blood as of a dead man" is not fresh blood but the thick, clotted blood of a corpse.\f*
\v 4 And the third poured out his bowl into the rivers and the springs of waters; and they became blood.
\v 5 And I heard the angel of the waters saying, "You are righteous, the One who is and who was, the holy one, because you judged these things;
\v 6 because they poured out the blood of holy ones and prophets, and you have given them blood to drink. They deserve it."\f + \fr 16:6 \ft The angel whose own element is being ruined declares the ruin just. And he says why: the punishment is the crime handed back. They poured out blood; they are given blood. It is the oldest rule of justice in the scriptures — "as you have done, it will be done to you" (Obadiah 15; Genesis 9:6).\f*
\v 7 And I heard the altar saying, "Yes, Lord God of hosts, true and righteous are your judgments."\f + \fr 16:7 \ft The altar under which the slaughtered cried "how long?" (6:9-10) speaks for itself. Their question has been answered and they say so.\f*
\v 8 And the fourth poured out his bowl on the sun; and it was given to it to burn men with fire.
\v 9 And men were burned with great burning, and they blasphemed the name of God who has the authority over these plagues, and they did not turn back to give him glory.\f + \fr 16:9 \ft They know exactly where the plagues come from. This is not ignorance but defiance, and it is Pharaoh's, blow after blow (Exodus 7-11). The first angel had called the earth to "fear God and give him glory" (14:7); here is the refusal, stated three times in this chapter (16:9, 11, 21).\f*
\v 10 And the fifth poured out his bowl on the throne of the beast; and his kingdom became darkened, and they chewed their tongues from the pain,\f + \fr 16:10 \ft Egypt's ninth plague, three days of darkness that could be felt — while "all the sons of Israel had light in their dwellings" (Exodus 10:21-23). And the target is precise: the throne the dragon gave him (13:2).\f*
\v 11 and they blasphemed the God of heaven because of their pains and because of their sores, and they did not turn back from their works.\add [i.e. the sores of the first bowl are still on them — nothing has been lifted, only added]\add*
\s1 The Sixth Bowl and the Gathering at Harmagedon
\p
\v 12 And the sixth poured out his bowl on the great river Euphrates; and its water was dried up, so that the way might be made ready for the kings from the rising of the sun.\f + \fr 16:12 \ft God dried the sea to make a road for his people out of Egypt (Exodus 14:21; Isaiah 51:10). Here the drying makes a road for invaders. Isaiah had said of Babylon that God says to the deep, "Be dry, and I will dry up your rivers" — and in the next breath names Cyrus, who came from the east (Isaiah 44:27-28); Herodotus reports that Cyrus took Babylon by diverting the Euphrates and walking his troops up the riverbed. The empire's eastern moat is removed.\f*
\v 13 And I saw coming out of the mouth of the dragon and out of the mouth of the beast and out of the mouth of the false prophet three unclean spirits like frogs;\f + \fr 16:13 \ft Egypt's second plague — frogs in the houses, the beds, the ovens, the kneading bowls, inescapable (Exodus 8:2-6). And the three figures are named together for the first time: dragon, beast, false prophet, a counterfeit of the Father, the Son, and the Spirit, each with his own mouth and the same output.\f*
\v 14 for they are spirits of demons doing signs, which go out to the kings of the whole inhabited world, to gather them to the war of the great day of the God of hosts.\f + \fr 16:14 \ft The spirits think they are mustering an army. In the prophets it is always God who gathers the nations, and he gathers them to be judged: "I will gather all the nations and bring them down to the valley of Jehoshaphat, and there I will enter into judgment with them" (Joel 3:2; Zechariah 14:2).\f*
\v 15 ("Look, I am coming like a thief. Blessed is the one who stays awake and keeps his garments, so that he may not walk naked and they see his shame.")\f + \fr 16:15 \ft Christ himself, breaking into the middle of the vision to speak straight to the listening assemblies — as at Sardis, in nearly the same words (3:2-3), and to Laodicea, who did not know she was naked (3:17). Even here, with the kings assembling, the instruction to the churches is the same: stay awake, keep your clothes on.\f*
\v 16 And they gathered them to the place called in Hebrew Harmagedon.\f + \fr 16:16 \ft Most naturally \tl har megiddo\tl*, the mountain of Megiddo — the pass through the Carmel range that controlled the great trade road, and the battlefield of Israel's memory. There the kings of Canaan fought and the stars fought against Sisera (Judges 5:19-20); there Josiah, the last good king, was killed, and the mourning for him became proverbial (2 Kings 23:29-30; Zechariah 12:11). Megiddo itself sits in a plain, not on a mountain; Ezekiel puts the last battle on "the mountains of Israel" (Ezekiel 39:2, 4). Note also that no battle is described here. They are gathered; the fighting waits for 19:19.\f*
\s1 The Seventh Bowl: It Has Happened
\p
\v 17 And the seventh poured out his bowl on the air; and a great voice came out of the sanctuary, from the throne, saying, "It has happened."\f + \fr 16:17 \ft One word in Greek, in the perfect tense: done, and standing done. The wrath announced as "finished" in 15:1 is finished. The same word will be spoken again in 21:6, over the new creation.\f*
\v 18 And there were lightnings and voices and thunders, and a great shaking happened, such as has not happened since man came to be on the earth — so great a shaking, so severe.\f + \fr 16:18 \ft The Sinai formula one last time, and at full strength. It began at the throne with three elements (4:5), gained an earthquake at 8:5, gained great hail at 11:19, and here reaches its limit. "Such as has not happened since man came to be" is Daniel's phrase for the final distress (Daniel 12:1).\f*
\v 19 And the great city became three parts, and the cities of the nations fell. And Babylon the great was remembered before God, to give her the cup of the wine of the fury of his anger.\f + \fr 16:19 \ft When God "remembers" in the scriptures he acts on what he remembers: he remembered Noah and the flood receded, he remembered his covenant and the exodus began (Genesis 8:1; Exodus 2:24). Babylon has now been remembered. What that means occupies the next two chapters.\f*
\v 20 And every island fled, and mountains were not found.
\v 21 And great hail, weighing about a talent, comes down out of heaven on men; and men blasphemed God because of the plague of the hail, because its plague is exceedingly great.\f + \fr 16:21 \ft Egypt's seventh plague, hail such as had not been seen since the land was founded (Exodus 9:18-26) — and at Gibeon, where the LORD threw great stones from heaven and more died from the hail than by Israel's swords (Joshua 10:11). A talent is something near a hundred pounds. And still the last word about these men is that they cursed God.\f*

\c 17
\s1 The Whore Seated on the Beast
\p
\v 1 And one of the seven angels having the seven bowls came and spoke with me, saying, "Come, I will show you the judgment of the great whore seated on many waters,\f + \fr 17:1 \ft Hold this sentence beside 21:9, where one of the same seven angels says, "Come, I will show you the bride, the wife of the Lamb." The wording is deliberately identical. Two women, two cities, shown by the same guide: this is the frame John has built for everything from here to the end. The prophets had already called cities whores — Tyre for her trade (Isaiah 23:15-17), Nineveh for her seductions (Nahum 3:4), Jerusalem herself for her alliances (Ezekiel 16; 23). "Seated on many waters" is Jeremiah's address to Babylon on her canals (Jeremiah 51:13); verse 15 will say what the waters are.\f*
\v 2 with whom the kings of the earth committed whoring, and those who dwell on the earth were made drunk from the wine of her whoring."\f + \fr 17:2 \ft "Babylon was a golden cup in the LORD's hand, making all the earth drunk; the nations drank of her wine, therefore the nations went mad" (Jeremiah 51:7). Drunk men cannot see straight, and that is the condition being described: the world cannot tell that what it is buying will kill it.\f*
\v 3 And he carried me off in spirit into a wilderness. And I saw a woman seated on a scarlet beast full of names of blasphemy, having seven heads and ten horns.\f + \fr 17:3 \ft In 12:6 the faithful woman fled to the wilderness and was fed there; in 21:10 John will be carried to a high mountain to see the bride. This woman is shown to him in the wasteland — the place of her own coming desolation. And she is riding: the beast of chapter 13 carries her, which is to say that her wealth and glamour sit on top of somebody else's army.\f*
\v 4 And the woman was clothed in purple and scarlet and gilded with gold and precious stone and pearls, holding a golden cup in her hand full of abominations and the unclean things of her whoring;\f + \fr 17:4 \ft Purple and scarlet were the most expensive dyes in the world and the colours of Roman rank; they are also two of the three colours of the tabernacle hangings (Exodus 26:1) — the third, the blue of heaven, is missing. The cup is gorgeous and the contents are filth. \tl Bdelygma\tl*, abomination, is the scriptures' word for idolatry (Deuteronomy 7:25-26). Compare the bride in 19:8, dressed in linen that is the righteous acts of the holy ones.\f*
\v 5 and on her forehead a name written, a secret: "Babylon the great, the mother of the whores and of the abominations of the earth."\f + \fr 17:5 \ft Roman prostitutes are reported to have worn their trade name on a band at the brow. She advertises what she is, in plain sight, and the world is too drunk to read it. "Mother" says she is not one instance among many but the source: every lesser imitation of her comes out of her.\f*
\v 6 And I saw the woman drunk from the blood of the holy ones and from the blood of the witnesses of Jesus. And when I saw her I marveled with great marveling.\f + \fr 17:6 \ft The turn of the whole vision. Under the gold and the pearls is blood, and she is drunk on it. And John's own reaction is the wrong one: "marvel" is the word used in 13:3 and 17:8 for what the earth-dwellers do when they look at the beast. Even the seer is briefly taken in by the spectacle, which is why the angel's next word is a correction.\f*
\s1 The Angel Explains the Beast
\p
\v 7 And the angel said to me, "Why did you marvel? I will tell you the secret of the woman and of the beast carrying her, the one having the seven heads and the ten horns.
\v 8 The beast that you saw was, and is not, and is about to come up out of the abyss and go off to ruin. And those dwelling on the earth will marvel — those whose name has not been written in the scroll of life from the founding of the world — when they see the beast, that he was and is not and will be present.\f + \fr 17:8 \ft A parody of the divine name. God is "the One who is and who was and who is coming" (1:4, 8; 4:8). The beast was, is not, and is about to come up — out of the pit, and on the way to ruin. Where God simply is, the beast has a hole in the middle of his existence; where God's coming is salvation, the beast's is a rise from the abyss to destruction. It looks like a resurrection and it ends in a grave.\f*
\v 9 Here is the mind that has wisdom. The seven heads are seven mountains, where the woman sits on them; and they are seven kings.\f + \fr 17:9 \ft No first-century hearer needed this decoded. Rome was the city on seven hills — the poets say so routinely, the coins show the goddess Roma seated on them, and the city kept an annual festival of the Seven Hills. The heads are both the place and the men who ruled from it.\f*
\v 10 Five fell, one is, the other has not yet come; and when he comes he must remain a little while.\f + \fr 17:10 \ft Counting emperors depends on where you start and whether you include the three who held power for months during the civil war of 68-69, and the text gives no key. Begin with Augustus and skip those three and the sixth is Vespasian and the seventh is Titus, who reigned barely two years — "a little while." What the sentence does for certain is place the readers late in the list. Only one more to come, and he does not last.\f*
\v 11 And the beast that was and is not, he himself is also an eighth, and is of the seven, and goes off to ruin.\add [i.e. more than any one of them, and yet embodied in one of them]\add*
\v 12 And the ten horns that you saw are ten kings, who have not yet received a kingdom, but they receive authority as kings for one hour with the beast.\f + \fr 17:12 \ft Daniel 7:24: "the ten horns are ten kings who will arise from this kingdom." "One hour" is the same measure the mourners will use for Babylon's fall (18:10, 17, 19). Their reign and her ruin occupy the same hour.\f*
\v 13 These have one mind, and they give their power and authority to the beast.\add [i.e. they hand their sovereignty over; nobody takes it from them]\add*
\v 14 These will make war with the Lamb, and the Lamb will conquer them, because he is Lord of lords and King of kings; and those with him are called and chosen and faithful."\f + \fr 17:14 \ft "The God of gods and the Lord of lords" is Deuteronomy 10:17; "God of gods and Lord of kings" is Daniel 2:47. The ten kings had to be given their authority; the Lamb has it. And note how his army is described — not by rank or arms but as called, chosen, and faithful. The first two are God's doing; the third is theirs.\f*
\s1 The Beast Turns on the Whore
\p
\v 15 And he says to me, "The waters that you saw, where the whore sits, are peoples and crowds and nations and tongues.\add [i.e. she is not sitting on a river; she is sitting on us]\add*
\v 16 And the ten horns that you saw, and the beast — these will hate the whore and will make her desolate and naked, and they will eat her flesh and burn her up with fire.\f + \fr 17:16 \ft The system devours its own. Stripping a faithless city naked is the prophets' standard picture of her exposure (Ezekiel 16:39; 23:26-29; Hosea 2:3); eating her flesh is the fate of Jezebel, whom the dogs ate in the plot at Jezreel (2 Kings 9:36); and burning is the sentence in the law on a priest's daughter who plays the whore (Leviticus 21:9). The empire she rode is the thing that ends her.\f*
\v 17 For God gave it into their hearts to do his mind, and to do one mind, and to give their kingdom to the beast, until the words of God are finished.\f + \fr 17:17 \ft The kings believe they are pursuing their own interest, and they are; and they are carrying out God's sentence while they do it. The same pattern runs through the older histories — God sends a spirit of ill-will between Abimelech and Shechem, and they destroy each other (Judges 9:23); the invaders at Jehoshaphat's door turn on one another and Israel never draws a sword (2 Chronicles 20:22-23).\f*
\v 18 And the woman that you saw is the great city that has kingship over the kings of the earth."\f + \fr 17:18 \ft The plain identification, and for the first hearers there was only one candidate. But John has called her Babylon, which is a type and not a postal address: the name belongs to any city that grows fat on other people's labour, seduces the nations with its glamour, demands the honour due to God, and silences whoever will not play along. Rome is the instance in front of them.\f*

\c 18
\s1 Fallen, Fallen Is Babylon
\p
\v 1 After these things I saw another angel coming down out of heaven, having great authority; and the earth was lit up from his glory.\f + \fr 18:1 \ft Ezekiel saw the glory of the God of Israel come from the east, "and the earth shone with his glory" (Ezekiel 43:2). This messenger carries so much of it that the announcement he makes is lit from above.\f*
\v 2 And he cried out in a strong voice, saying, "Fallen, fallen is Babylon the great, and she has become a dwelling of demons and a prison of every unclean spirit and a prison of every unclean bird and a prison of every unclean and hated beast;\f + \fr 18:2 \ft Isaiah's Babylon, after judgment: never inhabited again, "but desert creatures will lie there, and their houses will be full of howling creatures, and demons will dance there" (Isaiah 13:19-22; also 34:11-15; Jeremiah 50:39; 51:37). The city that housed the world's trade becomes a cage for everything the law called unclean.\f*
\v 3 because all the nations have drunk of the wine of the fury of her whoring, and the kings of the earth committed whoring with her, and the merchants of the earth grew rich from the power of her luxury."\f + \fr 18:3 \ft The merchant is new. Isaiah and Jeremiah indicted Babylon as a tyrant; Ezekiel indicted Tyre as a market (Ezekiel 27). John fuses the two, because the power he is describing is both — it rules and it sells. \tl Strēnos\tl* is not wealth but wanton excess, luxury that flaunts itself.\f*
\s1 Come Out of Her, My People
\p
\v 4 And I heard another voice out of heaven saying, "Come out of her, my people, so that you do not share in her sins and so that you do not receive any of her plagues;\f + \fr 18:4 \ft The command God gave through the prophets when the sentence on Babylon was about to fall: "Come out of her midst, my people, and save each man his life" (Jeremiah 51:45; also 50:8; Isaiah 48:20; 52:11). Two things follow from it. First, God's people are inside the system, or there would be nothing to come out of. Second, coming out is not emigration — no one is being told to leave the province — but disentanglement from the trade, the worship, and the appetites that hold the thing together.\f*
\v 5 because her sins have been heaped up as far as heaven, and God has remembered her wrongs.\f + \fr 18:5 \ft "Come, let us build a city and a tower whose top reaches to heaven" (Genesis 11:4). Babylon's first ambition was to reach heaven, and her sins have finally done it. Jeremiah says the same of her judgment: "it has reached to heaven and been lifted up to the clouds" (Jeremiah 51:9).\f*
\v 6 Pay her back as she also paid out, and double the double according to her works; in the cup she mixed, mix her double.\f + \fr 18:6 \ft "Repay her according to her work; according to all she has done, do to her" (Jeremiah 50:29). She held a cup and made the nations drink from it (17:4); a cup is now mixed for her.\f*
\v 7 As much as she glorified herself and lived in luxury, that much torment and grief give her. Because in her heart she says, 'I sit a queen, and I am not a widow, and I will never see grief,'\f + \fr 18:7 \ft Isaiah 47:7-8, of Babylon, almost word for word: "you said, I shall be a queen forever... you who say in your heart, I am, and there is no one besides me; I shall not sit as a widow, nor shall I know the loss of children." The arrogance is not new. It is the same voice Isaiah heard, in another empire's mouth.\f*
\v 8 for this in one day her plagues will come — death and grief and famine — and she will be burned up with fire, because strong is the Lord God who judged her.\f + \fr 18:8 \ft "These two things shall come upon you suddenly, in one day" (Isaiah 47:9). She said never; the answer is today. And the reason a city like this can fall in a day is not military but theological, and the verse says so: the one judging her is stronger.\f*
\s1 Kings, Merchants, and Sailors Mourn Their Market
\p
\v 9 And the kings of the earth who committed whoring and lived in luxury with her will weep and beat their breasts over her, when they see the smoke of her burning,\f + \fr 18:9 \ft Abraham looked toward Sodom and saw the smoke of the land going up (Genesis 19:28). Three groups now mourn in turn — kings, merchants, seafarers — and each keeps its distance and grieves its own loss. Not one of them goes into the wreckage.\f*
\v 10 standing far off because of the fear of her torment, saying, "Woe, woe, the great city, Babylon the strong city, because in one hour your judgment came."\f + \fr 18:10 \ft The princes of the sea come down from their thrones and tremble over fallen Tyre in the same posture (Ezekiel 26:16-17). And they call her strong — the word used of God in verse 8. Only one of the two claims survived the day.\f*
\v 11 And the merchants of the earth weep and mourn over her, because no one buys their cargo any more —\add [i.e. they are not weeping for her; they are weeping for the market]\add*
\v 12 cargo of gold and silver and precious stone and pearls and fine linen and purple and silk and scarlet, and every citron wood and every ivory vessel and every vessel of most precious wood and of bronze and iron and marble,\f + \fr 18:12 \ft Modelled on Ezekiel's inventory of Tyre's trade (Ezekiel 27:12-25), but the goods are Rome's. Silk came overland from China and cost its weight many times over in gold; purple was drawn drop by drop from shellfish; citron wood from north Africa made tabletops that Pliny says fetched the price of an estate. This is not commerce in necessities. It is the freight of appetite.\f*
\v 13 and cinnamon and spice and incense and perfume and frankincense and wine and oil and fine flour and wheat and cattle and sheep, and of horses and of carriages, and bodies — and souls of men.\f + \fr 18:13 \ft The list descends from jewels to spices to grain to livestock, and ends where it was always going to end. "Bodies" was the ordinary trade word for slaves, the term a shipping clerk would use. John writes it and then refuses to leave it standing: and souls of men. The whole catalogue of splendour is carried on that last entry.\f*
\v 14 And the ripe fruit your soul craved has gone from you, and all the rich and bright things have been destroyed from you, and they will never find them again.
\v 15 The merchants of these things, who grew rich from her, will stand far off because of the fear of her torment, weeping and mourning,
\v 16 saying, "Woe, woe, the great city, clothed in fine linen and purple and scarlet, and gilded with gold and precious stone and pearl,
\v 17 because in one hour so much wealth was made desolate." And every steersman and everyone sailing to a place, and sailors and as many as work the sea, stood far off\f + \fr 18:17 \ft They describe her exactly as John saw her in 17:4 — the same fabrics, the same stones. What they remember of her is the merchandise she wore.\f*
\v 18 and cried out when they saw the smoke of her burning, saying, "What is like the great city?"\f + \fr 18:18 \ft The third time this book puts the worship question in the wrong mouth. "Who is like the beast?" said the earth in 13:4; "who is like you, O LORD?" sang Israel at the sea (Exodus 15:11). Now it is said of a city.\f*
\v 19 And they threw dust on their heads and cried out, weeping and mourning, saying, "Woe, woe, the great city, in which all who had ships on the sea grew rich from her costliness, because in one hour she was made desolate."\f + \fr 18:19 \ft Ezekiel's sailors do precisely this over Tyre — come down from their ships, stand on the land, cry bitterly, and throw dust on their heads (Ezekiel 27:29-30). Throwing dust on the head is the oldest gesture of grief in the scriptures (Joshua 7:6; Job 2:12; Lamentations 2:10).\f*
\s1 The Millstone and the Silence
\p
\v 20 Be glad over her, heaven, and you holy ones and sent ones and prophets, because God has judged your case against her.\f + \fr 18:20 \ft The dirge is interrupted for one verse. Jeremiah issued the same summons over the first Babylon: "then heaven and earth and all that is in them will shout for joy over Babylon" (Jeremiah 51:48; also Deuteronomy 32:43). What is grief on earth is relief in heaven, and the difference between the two reactions is simply whose losses are being counted. This verse is also the answer to 6:10 — the case has been heard.\f*
\v 21 And one strong angel lifted a stone like a great millstone and threw it into the sea, saying, "So with violence Babylon the great city will be thrown down, and will never be found again.\f + \fr 18:21 \ft Jeremiah told Seraiah to read the oracle against Babylon aloud, tie a stone to the scroll, and throw it into the Euphrates, saying, "So shall Babylon sink and not rise again" (Jeremiah 51:63-64). John enlarges the stone to a millstone — the great one a donkey turns. What goes into deep water like that does not come back.\f*
\v 22 And the sound of harpists and musicians and flute players and trumpeters will never be heard in you again, and no craftsman of any craft will ever be found in you again, and the sound of a mill will never be heard in you again,\f + \fr 18:22 \ft The list that follows is Jeremiah 25:10 expanded: God takes from a doomed land "the voice of joy and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride, the sound of the millstones and the light of the lamp." Music, work, the grinding of grain, lamplight, weddings — every ordinary sound that says a place is still alive, switched off one by one.\f*
\v 23 and the light of a lamp will never shine in you again, and the voice of a bridegroom and bride will never be heard in you again; because your merchants were the great men of the earth, because all the nations were led astray by your sorcery,\f + \fr 18:23 \ft Two reasons given, and they belong together. Her merchants were her nobility — wealth was the ruling class. And \tl pharmakeia\tl*, sorcery, is what Nahum charged Nineveh with and Isaiah charged Babylon with (Nahum 3:4; Isaiah 47:9, 12): not conjuring tricks but the drugged persuasion by which an empire makes the world believe there is no other way to live.\f*
\v 24 and in her was found the blood of prophets and of holy ones and of all those slaughtered on the earth."\f + \fr 18:24 \ft The last word, and the widest. Not only the martyrs of these assemblies but everyone ever slaughtered — because what is being judged is not one city's police record but the principle of getting rich by killing, which has been running since Cain. Compare Jesus' charge that the blood of all the prophets from Abel onward would be required (Luke 11:50-51).\f*

\c 19
\s1 Heaven Says Hallelujah
\p
\v 1 After these things I heard something like a great voice of a great crowd in heaven, saying, "Hallelujah! The rescue and the glory and the power belong to our God,\f + \fr 19:1 \ft Hebrew \tl hallelu-Yah\tl*, "praise the LORD" — the word that opens and closes the last psalms of the Psalter (Psalms 146-150). It occurs four times in six verses here and nowhere else in the New Testament. Untranslated, it is the sound of the temple.\f*
\v 2 because true and righteous are his judgments; because he judged the great whore who ruined the earth with her whoring, and he avenged the blood of his slaves from her hand."\f + \fr 19:2 \ft The exact answer to the exact question. In 6:10 the slaughtered asked how long until God would judge and avenge their blood; here, in the same two verbs, he has judged and he has avenged. It is the longest-running thread in the book, and it is now tied off. "From her hand" echoes God's word to Jehu about avenging the prophets' blood at Jezebel's hand (2 Kings 9:7).\f*
\v 3 And a second time they have said, "Hallelujah! And her smoke goes up into the ages of the ages."\f + \fr 19:3 \ft Isaiah 34:10, of Edom: "its smoke shall go up forever." Not a fire still burning but a ruin that never gets rebuilt.\f*
\v 4 And the twenty-four elders and the four living creatures fell and bowed down to God, the one seated on the throne, saying, "Amen. Hallelujah."\add [i.e. it is so — praise the LORD]\add*
\v 5 And a voice came out from the throne, saying, "Praise our God, all his slaves, and those who fear him, the small and the great."\f + \fr 19:5 \ft Almost verbatim from the Hallelujah psalms: "Praise, O slaves of the LORD, praise the name of the LORD" (Psalm 113:1; 135:1), and "he will bless those who fear the LORD, the small and the great" (Psalm 115:13).\f*
\s1 The Wedding of the Lamb
\p
\v 6 And I heard something like the voice of a great crowd and like the sound of many waters and like the sound of strong thunders, saying, "Hallelujah! Because the Lord our God of hosts has begun to reign.\f + \fr 19:6 \ft The enthronement cry of the psalms — "the LORD reigns" (Psalms 93:1; 97:1; 99:1). Not that he was ever not sovereign, but that the powers contesting his rule have now been cleared away and it shows.\f*
\v 7 Let us rejoice and be glad and give him the glory, because the wedding of the Lamb has come, and his wife has made herself ready;\f + \fr 19:7 \ft God as husband to his people runs through the prophets: "your Maker is your husband" (Isaiah 54:5); "I will betroth you to me forever... in faithfulness" (Hosea 2:19-20). The harlot of chapter 17 was the counterfeit of this. Note the verb: she has made herself ready. Something was required of her, and she has done it.\f*
\v 8 and it was given to her to be clothed in fine linen, bright and clean" — for the fine linen is the righteous acts of the holy ones.\f + \fr 19:8 \ft Both halves stand. The dress is given — it is not earned or bought. And what it is made of is named: the righteous acts of the holy ones, the actual obedience of actual people. Grace supplies the garment; the community's faithfulness is the cloth. Set beside the whore, who bought her clothes (17:4), the contrast is not gift against effort but two completely different economies.\f*
\v 9 And he says to me, "Write: Blessed are those called to the wedding supper of the Lamb." And he says to me, "These are the true words of God."\f + \fr 19:9 \ft Isaiah's feast on the mountain, "for all peoples, a feast of rich food," at which God swallows up death forever (Isaiah 25:6-8). Keep this supper in mind: there is another one in verse 17, and every person in the book attends one or the other.\f*
\v 10 And I fell before his feet to bow down to him. And he says to me, "See that you do not. I am a fellow slave of you and of your brothers who hold the testimony of Jesus. Bow down to God. For the testimony of Jesus is the spirit of prophecy."\f + \fr 19:10 \ft The whole crisis of the book is worship: who gets the bowed knee. The beast demanded it, the earth gave it, and here the seer himself misdirects it and is stopped flat. The angel puts himself on John's own level — a fellow slave, not a superior being. And the last line explains why no messenger may be worshipped: prophecy has no content of its own; it exists to point at Jesus.\f*
\s1 The Rider Called Faithful and True
\p
\v 11 And I saw heaven opened, and look — a white horse, and the one sitting on it is called Faithful and True, and in righteousness he judges and makes war.\f + \fr 19:11 \ft In 4:1 there was a door open in heaven; now heaven itself is open, and what comes through it comes down. "Faithful and true" is how the risen Christ named himself to Laodicea (3:14). Isaiah's coming king "judges with righteousness" and "strikes the earth with the rod of his mouth" (Isaiah 11:4-5) — every feature of this rider is in that passage.\f*
\v 12 And his eyes are a flame of fire, and on his head many diadems, having a name written that no one knows except himself,\f + \fr 19:12 \ft The dragon wore seven diadems (12:3) and the beast ten (13:1). This rider wears more than can be counted. And there is a name no one else knows — something of who he is that no creature can get hold of or use.\f*
\v 13 and he is clothed in a garment dipped in blood; and his name is called The Word of God.\f + \fr 19:13 \ft The robe is bloody before the battle starts. Isaiah's warrior comes back from treading the winepress with his garments spattered by the blood of those he trod (Isaiah 63:1-3), and verse 15 makes that connection explicit. But the Lamb also conquered by his own blood (5:9; 12:11), and he still bears the marks of it. Both are in the picture; neither cancels the other.\f*
\v 14 And the armies in heaven were following him on white horses, clothed in fine linen, white and clean.\f + \fr 19:14 \ft They are wearing the bride's dress of verse 8, which is to say the righteous acts of the holy ones. And they carry nothing. No swords are mentioned, no armour, no orders. They follow; he fights.\f*
\v 15 And out of his mouth goes a sharp sword, so that with it he may strike the nations; and he himself will shepherd them with an iron staff; and he himself treads the winepress of the wine of the fury of the anger of the God of hosts.\f + \fr 19:15 \ft The weapon has been in his mouth since 1:16. He does not swing anything; he speaks, and it is done — "he will strike the earth with the rod of his mouth, and with the breath of his lips he will slay the wicked" (Isaiah 11:4). The iron staff is Psalm 2:9, promised to the overcomer at Thyatira (2:27) and to the child in 12:5, and here used. The winepress is the vintage of 14:19-20, now trodden by him personally.\f*
\v 16 And he has on his garment and on his thigh a name written: King of kings and Lord of lords.\f + \fr 19:16 \ft Written where a rider's thigh shows above the horse — the one name of the four that is meant to be read from the ground. In Deuteronomy 10:17 and Daniel 2:47 the title belongs to God. The ten kings had to be given their authority (17:12-13); the one wearing this does not.\f*
\s1 The Other Supper
\p
\v 17 And I saw one angel standing in the sun, and he cried out in a great voice, saying to all the birds flying in midheaven, "Come, gather to the great supper of God,\f + \fr 19:17 \ft Ezekiel 39:17-20, after Gog's defeat: "Say to every bird and to every beast of the field, Assemble and come, gather to my sacrifice, that you may eat flesh and drink blood — the flesh of mighty men, the blood of the princes of the earth." Two suppers stand in this one chapter: the wedding in verse 9, where the guests eat, and this one, where the guests are eaten.\f*
\v 18 to eat flesh of kings and flesh of commanders and flesh of the strong and flesh of horses and of those sitting on them, and flesh of all, free and slaves and small and great."\f + \fr 19:18 \ft The same ranks that hid in the caves at the sixth seal (6:15) and the same ranks the beast branded (13:16). Before the birds, a king's flesh and a slave's flesh are the same commodity.\f*
\v 19 And I saw the beast and the kings of the earth and their armies gathered to make war with the one sitting on the horse and with his army.\add [i.e. the war they were mustered for at Harmagedon]\add*
\v 20 And the beast was seized, and with him the false prophet who did the signs in his presence, by which he led astray those who received the mark of the beast and those bowing down to his image. The two were thrown alive into the lake of fire burning with sulfur.\f + \fr 19:20 \ft There is no battle. The word for what happens to the beast is the word for an arrest — the same verb used of the seizing of Jesus. This is a police action, not a campaign. And the lake of fire is Sodom's fire and sulfur (Genesis 19:24), which in Israel's memory is the standard for a destruction that is total and never reversed.\f*
\v 21 And the rest were killed by the sword of the one sitting on the horse, the sword going out of his mouth; and all the birds were filled from their flesh.\add [i.e. killed by what he said]\add*

\c 20
\s1 The Dragon Bound for a Thousand Years
\p
\v 1 And I saw an angel coming down out of heaven, having the key of the abyss and a great chain in his hand.\f + \fr 20:1 \ft The beast and the false prophet went into the fire in 19:20; the one who gave them their power is still loose. This chapter deals with him. The key is delegated — the risen Christ holds the keys (1:18) — and binding rebel spirits with chains and shutting them in darkness is the standard picture in Jewish writing of the period (compare Jude 6; 2 Peter 2:4).\f*
\v 2 And he seized the dragon, the ancient serpent, who is Devil and Satan, and bound him a thousand years,\f + \fr 20:2 \ft The same four names as 12:9, so that no one can mistake which figure this is. A thousand in the scriptures is a full and sufficient measure, not a stopwatch reading — "a thousand years in your sight are like yesterday" (Psalm 90:4); "the cattle on a thousand hills" (Psalm 50:10). This book's other numbers all work that way.\f*
\v 3 and threw him into the abyss and shut it and sealed it over him, so that he should not lead the nations astray any more until the thousand years were finished. After these things he must be loosed a little time.\f + \fr 20:3 \ft Three verbs, each tighter than the last: thrown, shut, sealed. And the stated purpose is specific — not that he should stop existing but that he should stop deceiving, which is the one thing this book says he does. Note also "must": his release is not a failure of the lock but part of the plan.\f*
\s1 The First Resurrection
\p
\v 4 And I saw thrones, and they sat on them, and judgment was given to them; and the souls of those beheaded with the axe because of the testimony of Jesus and because of the word of God, and those who did not bow down to the beast or to his image and did not receive the mark on their forehead and on their hand — and they came to life and reigned with the Christ a thousand years.\f + \fr 20:4 \ft Daniel 7:9, 22: thrones were set in place, and "judgment was given for the holy ones of the Most High, and the time came when they took possession of the kingdom." The people described are the ones the book has been following all along — the souls under the altar (6:9), the ones who would not take the mark (13:15-17), the hundred forty-four thousand with the other name on their foreheads (14:1). The beast conquered them (13:7). Here they are on thrones. Beheading was Rome's execution for citizens.\f*
\v 5 The rest of the dead did not come to life until the thousand years were finished. This is the first resurrection.\add [i.e. first, and so implying a second — the general raising of verses 12-13]\add*
\v 6 Blessed and holy is the one having a part in the first resurrection. Over these the second death has no authority, but they will be priests of God and of the Christ, and they will reign with him the thousand years.\f + \fr 20:6 \ft Two deaths and two resurrections, laid out in a pattern. The faithful die the first death — that is what the beast can do to them — and rise in the first resurrection, and the second death has no jurisdiction over them. The promise to Smyrna in 2:11 is here cashed. And "priests of God" is the standing declaration of 1:6 and 5:10, made good at last: the men the empire executed are the priesthood.\f*
\s1 The Last Deception and the End of the Devil
\p
\v 7 And when the thousand years are finished, Satan will be loosed out of his prison,
\v 8 and he will go out to lead astray the nations at the four corners of the earth, Gog and Magog, to gather them to the war; their number is like the sand of the sea.\f + \fr 20:8 \ft Ezekiel 38-39: Gog, of the land of Magog, comes with a coalition of nations against a people living securely in a restored land, and God destroys the whole army with fire and sulfur from heaven. John takes Ezekiel's single northern enemy and makes the name stand for the nations of all four corners. And note what Satan does the instant he is free. Nothing has changed in him; he goes straight back to deceiving.\f*
\v 9 And they came up over the breadth of the earth and surrounded the camp of the holy ones and the beloved city; and fire came down out of heaven and devoured them.\f + \fr 20:9 \ft "Camp" is the word for Israel's wilderness encampment around the tabernacle — the holy ones are still pilgrims, gathered around a presence. The odds are hopeless and there is no battle: fire falls, as it fell on Sodom (Genesis 19:24) and on the men sent to arrest Elijah (2 Kings 1:10), and as Ezekiel said it would fall on Gog (Ezekiel 38:22).\f*
\v 10 And the devil who led them astray was thrown into the lake of fire and sulfur, where the beast and the false prophet also are; and they will be tormented day and night into the ages of the ages.\add [i.e. the three of them together at last — the dragon, and the two he sent]\add*
\s1 The Great White Throne
\p
\v 11 And I saw a great white throne and the one seated on it, from whose face the earth and the heaven fled, and no place was found for them.\f + \fr 20:11 \ft Earth and sky cannot stand in front of him and run for it. "No place was found for them" is the phrase used of the shattered image in Nebuchadnezzar's dream, blown away without a trace (Daniel 2:35). This is why 21:1 can begin with a new heaven and a new earth: the old ones have just left.\f*
\v 12 And I saw the dead, the great and the small, standing before the throne; and scrolls were opened. And another scroll was opened, which is of life. And the dead were judged from the things written in the scrolls, according to their works.\f + \fr 20:12 \ft "The court sat, and the books were opened" (Daniel 7:10). Two kinds of book, doing two different jobs. The books of deeds are the evidence: God keeps a record, and nothing done is forgotten (Malachi 3:16; Psalm 56:8). The scroll of life is the register of those who are his (Exodus 32:32-33; Daniel 12:1). This book has held both together from the beginning — "I know your works" to every assembly, and a name that can be blotted out or kept (3:5).\f*
\v 13 And the sea gave up the dead in it, and Death and Hades gave up the dead in them; and each was judged according to their works.\add [i.e. even those the sea swallowed and no one buried — no death is too obscure to be reached]\add*
\v 14 And Death and Hades were thrown into the lake of fire. This is the second death — the lake of fire.\f + \fr 20:14 \ft The pale rider and what followed him (6:8) end in the fire themselves. Death has been the dragon's only real weapon in this book — it killed the Lamb, the witnesses, and the martyrs — and here it is destroyed. "He will swallow up death forever" (Isaiah 25:8). After this verse death does not appear again except to be denied (21:4).\f*
\v 15 And if anyone was not found written in the scroll of life, he was thrown into the lake of fire.\add [i.e. the register is searched, name by name]\add*

\c 21
\s1 A New Heaven, a New Earth, and God Living With Men
\p
\v 1 And I saw a new heaven and a new earth; for the first heaven and the first earth went away, and the sea is no more.\f + \fr 21:1 \ft "Behold, I create new heavens and a new earth, and the former things shall not be remembered" (Isaiah 65:17; 66:22). The Greek word for new here means new in kind, not merely recent. And the sea is gone — not a comment on geography. The sea in this book is where chaos lives and where the beast came from (13:1), and the sea of glass was the barrier between the throne and everything below it (4:6). Every separation is finished.\f*
\v 2 And I saw the holy city, new Jerusalem, coming down out of heaven from God, made ready as a bride adorned for her husband.\f + \fr 21:2 \ft Promised to the overcomer at Philadelphia in almost these words (3:12). The direction matters: the city comes down. Men built a tower to get up to heaven (Genesis 11:4); God brings his dwelling down to them. And she is a bride — the same figure as 19:7, seen now as a city. Isaiah 61:10: "as a bride adorns herself with her jewels."\f*
\v 3 And I heard a great voice from the throne saying, "Look, the tent of God is with men, and he will tent with them, and they will be his peoples, and God himself will be with them, their God.\f + \fr 21:3 \ft The covenant sentence, in its oldest form: "I will set my dwelling among you... I will be your God, and you shall be my people" (Leviticus 26:11-12; Jeremiah 31:33; Ezekiel 37:27). "Tent" is the wilderness tabernacle — the portable dwelling by which God travelled with a people who had no country yet. Note the plural in some manuscripts: his peoples. The covenant made with one nation now takes in many.\f*
\v 4 And he will wipe away every tear from their eyes, and death will be no more, nor will grief nor crying nor pain be any more, because the first things went away."\f + \fr 21:4 \ft Isaiah 25:8, at the feast on the mountain: "he will swallow up death forever, and the Lord GOD will wipe away tears from all faces." The promise was already made to the great crowd in 7:17; here it is done. And it is done by hand — God wipes the face himself.\f*
\v 5 And the one seated on the throne said, "Look, I am making all things new." And he says, "Write, because these words are faithful and true."\f + \fr 21:5 \ft One of the very few places in the book where God speaks directly. "Behold, I am doing a new thing" (Isaiah 43:19) — said there of the return from exile, said here of everything.\f*
\v 6 And he said to me, "They have happened. I am the Alpha and the Omega, the beginning and the end. To the one thirsting I will give from the spring of the water of life freely.\f + \fr 21:6 \ft The same word that ended the bowls in 16:17, now spoken over the new creation. And the invitation is Isaiah's: "everyone who thirsts, come to the waters; you who have no money, come, buy without money and without price" (Isaiah 55:1). Under the beast a man could not buy so much as bread without the mark (13:17). Here the one thing that matters costs nothing.\f*
\v 7 The one who conquers will inherit these things, and I will be God to him and he will be a son to me.\f + \fr 21:7 \ft Every promise made to the seven assemblies is gathered into that one word, "these things." And the covenant God made with David's heir — "I will be his father, and he shall be my son" (2 Samuel 7:14) — is handed to whoever holds out.\f*
\v 8 But for the cowardly and untrusting and detestable and murderers and whoremongers and sorcerers and idolaters and all the liars, their part is in the lake burning with fire and sulfur, which is the second death."\f + \fr 21:8 \ft The list is headed, strikingly, by the cowardly — because in a book about refusing the beast's mark on pain of starvation and death, cowardice is not a temperament but a decision. The law sent the fearful man home from the army lest he melt his brothers' hearts (Deuteronomy 20:8). Everything else on the list is the beast's system named piece by piece: its violence, its idolatry, its drugged persuasion, and the lie at the bottom of it.\f*
\s1 The Bride Shown as a City
\p
\v 9 And one of the seven angels having the seven bowls full of the seven last plagues came and spoke with me, saying, "Come, I will show you the bride, the wife of the Lamb."\f + \fr 21:9 \ft Word for word the invitation of 17:1, with the object changed. The same class of angel, the same summons, the other woman. Everything from here on is meant to be read against Babylon.\f*
\v 10 And he carried me off in spirit onto a great and high mountain, and he showed me the holy city Jerusalem coming down out of heaven from God,\f + \fr 21:10 \ft To see the whore he was taken into a wilderness (17:3); to see the bride he is taken up a mountain. Ezekiel was set "on a very high mountain, and on it a structure like a city" (Ezekiel 40:2), and everything that follows here is built on Ezekiel 40-48 — with one enormous alteration, which comes at verse 22.\f*
\v 11 having the glory of God. Her radiance was like a most precious stone, like jasper stone, clear as crystal;\f + \fr 21:11 \ft Jasper is how the one on the throne was described in 4:3. The city shines with the same light as its maker — Ezekiel's glory, which left the temple before its ruin and came back at the end (Ezekiel 10:18-19; 43:2-5), no longer shut in a building.\f*
\v 12 having a great and high wall, having twelve gates, and at the gates twelve angels, and names written on them, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the sons of Israel:\f + \fr 21:12 \ft Ezekiel's city has exactly this: twelve gates, three to a side, each named for a tribe (Ezekiel 48:30-34). Whoever comes into this city comes in through Israel. And the angels at the gates are not barring them — verse 25 says the gates never close.\f*
\v 13 from the east three gates, and from the north three gates, and from the south three gates, and from the west three gates.\add [i.e. a way in from every direction on earth]\add*
\v 14 And the wall of the city has twelve foundations, and on them twelve names of the twelve sent ones of the Lamb.\f + \fr 21:14 \ft Twelve tribes on the gates, twelve sent ones on the foundations, and the gates rest on the foundations. Israel's covenant history and the apostolic testimony are not two buildings but one, and the joints are structural.\f*
\v 15 And the one speaking with me had a golden reed as a measure, so that he might measure the city and its gates and its wall.\f + \fr 21:15 \ft Ezekiel's guide had a measuring reed and used it for three chapters (Ezekiel 40:3-5); Zechariah's man went to measure Jerusalem and was told the city would need no walls, because God himself would be a wall of fire around her (Zechariah 2:1-5). What God measures, God claims.\f*
\v 16 And the city lies foursquare, and its length is as much as the breadth. And he measured the city with the reed at twelve thousand stadia; its length and breadth and height are equal.\f + \fr 21:16 \ft Equal in all three dimensions: a cube. Exactly one thing in Israel's sacred architecture was a cube — the innermost room of Solomon's temple, twenty cubits by twenty by twenty (1 Kings 6:20), where the ark stood and where one man went one day a year. The whole city is now that room. Twelve thousand stadia is some fourteen hundred miles a side, which is not a survey figure but the covenant number twelve multiplied out until it is big enough to hold everyone.\f*
\v 17 And he measured its wall, a hundred forty-four cubits, by a man's measure, which is an angel's.\f + \fr 21:17 \ft Twelve squared. Set against a city fourteen hundred miles across, a wall of two hundred feet is no defence at all — which is the point. It marks a boundary; it does not hold anyone off. The closing note assures the reader that the angel's measurements convert into human units.\f*
\v 18 And the material of its wall was jasper, and the city was pure gold, like clear glass.\add [i.e. gold you can see through — nothing in the structure blocks the light inside it]\add*
\v 19 The foundations of the wall of the city were adorned with every precious stone: the first foundation jasper, the second sapphire, the third chalcedony, the fourth emerald,
\v 20 the fifth sardonyx, the sixth carnelian, the seventh chrysolite, the eighth beryl, the ninth topaz, the tenth chrysoprase, the eleventh jacinth, the twelfth amethyst.\f + \fr 21:19-20 \ft Twelve stones, most of them the twelve of the high priest's breastpiece, each of which carried the engraved name of a tribe and was worn over the heart into God's presence (Exodus 28:17-20). Isaiah had promised Jerusalem foundations of sapphire and battlements of jasper (Isaiah 54:11-12), and Ezekiel says every precious stone was the covering in Eden (Ezekiel 28:13). Temple, city, and garden are being described at once.\f*
\v 21 And the twelve gates were twelve pearls; each one of the gates was of one pearl. And the street of the city was pure gold, like transparent glass.\f + \fr 21:21 \ft Pearls were among Babylon's luxuries — she wore them (17:4), her merchants shipped them, and they wept when the trade stopped (18:12, 16). Here the same commodity is a gate that anyone may walk through. The materials are not different; what they are for is.\f*
\s1 No Sanctuary, No Sun, No Night
\p
\v 22 And I saw no sanctuary in it, for the Lord God of hosts is its sanctuary, and the Lamb.\f + \fr 21:22 \ft This is the great departure from Ezekiel, who gave nine chapters to the measurements and regulations of the temple. There is no building here. A sanctuary exists to hold a presence and to manage access to it; when the presence is simply there and access is open, the building has nothing left to do.\f*
\v 23 And the city has no need of the sun or of the moon to shine on it, for the glory of God lit it up, and its lamp is the Lamb.\f + \fr 21:23 \ft "The sun shall no longer be your light by day, nor the moon by night; the LORD will be your everlasting light" (Isaiah 60:19-20). The created lights are not destroyed; they are simply not needed, as the sanctuary is not needed. Every middleman between God and his people has been removed. And in fallen Babylon "the light of a lamp will never shine in you again" (18:23); here the lamp is the Lamb.\f*
\v 24 And the nations will walk by its light, and the kings of the earth bring their glory into it.\f + \fr 21:24 \ft Isaiah 60:3, 11: "nations shall walk by your light, and kings by the brightness of your rising... your gates shall be open continually, that the wealth of nations may be brought in." The kings of the earth have been villains for nineteen chapters. Here they are carrying tribute in. Whatever was worth anything in the nations is not destroyed but brought home.\f*
\v 25 And its gates will never be shut by day — for there will be no night there.\add [i.e. gates are shut against darkness and enemies, and there is neither]\add*
\v 26 And they will bring the glory and the honor of the nations into it.
\v 27 And nothing common will ever enter into it, nor anyone doing abomination and lying, but only those written in the Lamb's scroll of life.\f + \fr 21:27 \ft Not a guard on the gate but a statement of what the place is. "Common" is the ritual word for what has not been set apart — the opposite of holy. A city that is entirely the innermost room of the temple cannot contain what is unclean, any more than light can contain darkness.\f*

\c 22
\s1 The River and the Tree of Life
\p
\v 1 And he showed me a river of water of life, bright as crystal, coming out of the throne of God and of the Lamb.\f + \fr 22:1 \ft Ezekiel saw water coming out from under the threshold of the temple, deepening as it ran until it could not be crossed, and everything lived where it went (Ezekiel 47:1-12). There is no temple here, so it comes from the throne. And the throne is one throne, God's and the Lamb's together.\f*
\v 2 In the middle of its street, and on this side and that of the river, a tree of life bearing twelve fruits, giving its fruit each month; and the leaves of the tree are for the healing of the nations.\f + \fr 22:2 \ft Ezekiel's river had many trees on both banks, bearing fruit every month, "their fruit for food and their leaves for healing" (Ezekiel 47:12). John makes them one tree, and names it: the tree in the middle of the garden, barred to man and guarded by cherubim and a turning sword (Genesis 2:9; 3:22-24). It was promised to the overcomer in the first of the seven letters (2:7). Also note where it stands — in the middle of the street, the most public place there is.\f*
\v 3 And there will be no more curse. And the throne of God and of the Lamb will be in it, and his slaves will serve him,\f + \fr 22:3 \ft "There will no longer be any curse" is Zechariah 14:11. What is lifted is the sentence of Genesis 3:14-19 — the cursed ground, the pain, the sweat, the death — and with it every consequence the book has been undoing since 21:1.\f*
\v 4 and they will see his face, and his name will be on their foreheads.\f + \fr 22:4 \ft The one thing that could not be had. "You cannot see my face, for no man can see me and live" (Exodus 33:20); Moses was put in a cleft of the rock and shown God's back. The priestly blessing asked only that God's face be turned toward Israel (Numbers 6:25-26). Here they look at it. And the high priest's golden plate on the forehead, engraved "Holy to the LORD" (Exodus 28:36), is now on everyone.\f*
\v 5 And night will be no more, and they have no need of lamplight or sunlight, because the Lord God will shine on them; and they will reign into the ages of the ages.\f + \fr 22:5 \ft The last word on the promise that began in 1:6 — a kingdom and priests. In 5:10 they would reign on the earth; in 20:4, 6 they reigned a thousand years; here the term is the same one used of God's own existence. The reign has no end.\f*
\s1 These Words Are Faithful and True
\p
\v 6 And he said to me, "These words are faithful and true. And the Lord, the God of the spirits of the prophets, sent his angel to show his slaves what must happen quickly.\f + \fr 22:6 \ft The book closes the circle it opened: "to show his slaves what must happen quickly" is 1:1 word for word. "The God of the spirits of the prophets" claims for this document the same source as Isaiah and Ezekiel — the impulse behind prophecy is God's, and it answers to him (compare 1 Corinthians 14:32).\f*
\v 7 And look, I am coming quickly. Blessed is the one who keeps the words of the prophecy of this scroll."\f + \fr 22:7 \ft The speaker changes to Jesus without warning, and says "I am coming quickly" three times before the book ends (22:7, 12, 20). And the blessing is the same as the one in 1:3, with the same verb: keep. The book is bracketed by a demand for obedience, not for admiration.\f*
\s1 John Falls Before the Angel Again
\p
\v 8 And I, John, am the one hearing and seeing these things. And when I heard and saw, I fell to bow down before the feet of the angel showing me these things.\f + \fr 22:8 \ft The second time, after being corrected once already in 19:10. That is not a copyist's slip; it is the point. If the man who has seen all of this can still misdirect his worship twice, so can anyone, and the assemblies who lived inside the imperial cult needed telling twice.\f*
\v 9 And he says to me, "See that you do not. I am a fellow slave of you and of your brothers the prophets and of those keeping the words of this scroll. Bow down to God."\add [i.e. angel, prophet, and ordinary obedient hearer are all in the same rank — and none of them is God]\add*
\v 10 And he says to me, "Do not seal up the words of the prophecy of this scroll, for the time is near.\f + \fr 22:10 \ft The exact reverse of Daniel's instruction — "shut up the words and seal the book until the time of the end" (Daniel 12:4, 9). Daniel sealed his because the time was far off. John is forbidden to seal his because it is not.\f*
\v 11 Let the one doing wrong still do wrong, and let the filthy one still be filthy, and let the righteous one still do righteousness, and let the holy one still be made holy."\f + \fr 22:11 \ft Not permission but finality. The moment of decision has arrived, and the four sentences say that people will be fixed in the direction they have chosen. Daniel says the same at the end of his own book: "many shall be purified and refined, but the wicked shall act wickedly" (Daniel 12:10).\f*
\s1 I Am Coming Quickly
\p
\v 12 "Look, I am coming quickly, and my wage is with me, to give back to each as his work is.\f + \fr 22:12 \ft "Behold, the Lord comes with might; behold, his reward is with him" (Isaiah 40:10; 62:11). He does not arrive to find out what people did and then decide; he arrives with the payment already in hand. And the measure is the one used on every assembly in chapters 2-3 and at the throne in 20:12-13: works.\f*
\v 13 I am the Alpha and the Omega, the first and the last, the beginning and the end.\f + \fr 22:13 \ft Three pairs of titles, gathered from across the book. God spoke the first in 1:8 and the third in 21:6; Jesus spoke the second in 1:17. Here he says all three. The book never argues the identification; it simply lets the titles travel.\f*
\v 14 Blessed are those who wash their robes, so that they may have the right to the tree of life and may enter into the city by the gates.\f + \fr 22:14 \ft The seventh and last blessing of the book. "They washed their robes and made them white in the blood of the Lamb" (7:14) — and the participle here is continuous: those who are washing them, as a way of living, not once and done. Compare the few at Sardis who had not soiled theirs (3:4).\f*
\v 15 Outside are the dogs and the sorcerers and the whoremongers and the murderers and the idolaters and everyone loving and doing a lie.\f + \fr 22:15 \ft "Dog" was a hard word: the law uses it of the male cult prostitute whose earnings could not come into God's house (Deuteronomy 23:18). The rest of the list is the beast's world named again (compare 21:8), ending where it began — with the lie, since the dragon's trade is deception.\f*
\v 16 I, Jesus, sent my angel to testify these things to you for the assemblies. I am the root and the offspring of David, the bright morning star."\f + \fr 22:16 \ft Root and offspring both: he is the stock David grew from and the shoot that came out of him (Isaiah 11:1, 10). And the morning star is what he promised to give the overcomer at Thyatira (2:28) — a promise that turns out to be himself. Behind it stands the one true oracle Balaam managed: "a star shall come out of Jacob" (Numbers 24:17). The teaching of Balaam was condemned in 2:14; his star ends the book.\f*
\s1 Come
\p
\v 17 And the Spirit and the bride say, "Come." And let the one hearing say, "Come." And let the one thirsting come; let the one wanting take the water of life freely.\f + \fr 22:17 \ft The word faces two ways at once. It is addressed upward to Christ — verse 20 will say it plainly — and outward to anyone listening. After twenty-two chapters of judgment the last movement of the book is an open invitation, and its only conditions are thirst and willingness. Isaiah 55:1: "come, buy without money and without price." Under the beast, nothing could be bought without his mark. Here nothing has to be bought at all.\f*
\v 18 I testify to everyone hearing the words of the prophecy of this scroll: if anyone adds to them, God will add to him the plagues written in this scroll;
\v 19 and if anyone takes away from the words of the scroll of this prophecy, God will take away his part from the tree of life and from the holy city, from the things written in this scroll.\f + \fr 22:18-19 \ft The formula that guarded the Torah: "you shall not add to the word which I command you, nor take away from it" (Deuteronomy 4:2; 12:32). The penalties match the offences exactly — add, and additions are made to you; subtract, and your own share is subtracted. It protects the text from copyists, and it protects the message from anyone minded to file off its hard edges.\f*
\v 20 The one testifying these things says, "Yes, I am coming quickly." Amen. Come, Lord Jesus.\f + \fr 22:20 \ft The oldest prayer the assemblies had. Paul writes it in Aramaic without translating it, assuming his Greek readers know it — \tl marana tha\tl*, "our Lord, come" (1 Corinthians 16:22), and it stands in the eucharistic prayer of the Didache. Everything the book has shown hangs on this one event, and the last thing the community says is a request for it.\f*
\v 21 The favor of the Lord Jesus be with all.\f + \fr 22:21 \ft The ending of a letter, matching the greeting of 1:4 — favor and peace at the front, favor at the back. Everything between, the judgments and the terrors and the demands, is enclosed by it. And it is extended to all, as widely as the water of life in verse 17.\f*
